//                ·  v9 2026-09-06 (tick #16, the TARGET RULE's feedback loop — FINDING 27)
//
// ⚠ v9 ADDS NO RULE THAT IS ON BY DEFAULT. Its one new input defaults to the v1-v8 behaviour, so
//   with default inputs its signal set and trade record are IDENTICAL to v8's. What it adds is the
//   measurement of a defect that is a THEOREM about this file, not an estimate:
//
//     Every closed trade's recorded R is bounded above by the target it was opened against.
//     A win exits AT `tgtR` and books exactly `tgtR`; a stop, a trail exit and v8's EOD flatten all
//     book LESS. So `mean(window) <= tgtR`, so `round(mean) <= tgtR`, so the adaptive target is a
//     MONOTONICALLY NON-INCREASING integer sequence floored at 1. It can never rise. And 1R is
//     strictly absorbing: at a 1R target a win books 1, the mean cannot exceed 1, and 1.5 — the
//     mean needed to round back up to 2 — is unreachable at any win rate.
//
//   Holding a target T therefore needs mean >= T - 0.5, i.e. a win rate of at least 1 - 0.5/T:
//   75% to hold 1:2, 83.3% to hold 1:3, 90% to hold 1:5. The BREAK-EVEN win rates at those targets
//   are 33.3%, 25% and 16.7%. A configuration that is comfortably profitable at 1:3 on a 40% win
//   rate ratchets to 1:1 within two windows, where 40% is a LOSS. The rule as implemented converts
//   a winning system into a losing one, and the pre-registered "rolling-mean vs fixed target" test
//   would measure that ratchet rather than his rule.
//
//   IT IS THIS FILE'S DEFECT, NOT HIS. His rule climbs — "One to five gets hit, three days in a
//   row. Okay, the average is now one to four" (10. 05:43) — and his journal set {3, 0, 2.5, 5, 5, 3}
//   averages 3.08 while containing two 5s, so a trade CAPTURED more R than the target in force. He
//   records "the risk to reward that I was able to CAPTURE" (10. 00:58), which is the furthest rung
//   the ladder reached, not the level he exited a single unit at. A simulator that closes the whole
//   position AT the target cannot represent that, whatever it feeds the estimator — capping capture
//   at the target is what breaks the loop, not the choice of window. Tick #8 filed the missing
//   scale-out as a cosmetic limitation ("the ladder is drawn, not traded"). It is not cosmetic; it
//   is the thing that turns a self-correcting estimator into a one-way ratchet. NOT FIXED HERE: the
//   only source-expressible repair is to stop exiting at the target and let a runner go on the
//   trail, and the source never says whether he holds one unit or scales out of several — inventing
//   weights would push a fabricated number into the target rule itself. Instrumented and named.
//
//   The one new input is the OTHER thing this tick found in the same module: `rEstimator`. He
//   DESCRIBES a rolling window ("the last two weeks of trades", 10. 00:40) and DEMONSTRATES an
//   expanding one — 18.5 + 2 = 20.5 "divided by now SEVEN trades" (10. 05:11), adding the new trade
//   without dropping the oldest. v1-v8 implement the described estimator with a drop-oldest rule the
//   source never states. Both are now available; the default is unchanged. Note the theorem above
//   holds under EITHER, which is the point of offering it — the ratchet is caused by the capped
//   capture, not by the window shape.
//
// ⚠ v8 ADDS NO RULE THAT IS ON BY DEFAULT. Both new switches ship OFF, so with default inputs its
//   signal set and trade record are IDENTICAL to v7's. What it adds is the measurement of a number
//   that has been in this file since v1 and was never sourced: the session END.
//   `sessTime` has read "0930-1600" since v1 — 390 minutes. Module 8 states the session START four
//   different ways ("930 a.m. Eastern", "6 30 a.m. Pacific", "be on 20 minutes early", "you don't
//   log on at 6 30 and just start trading") and NEVER states an end. 1600 is the RTH close, which
//   is a fact about the exchange, not a rule he gives. Meanwhile every day the corpus actually
//   records is over within ~20 minutes of the open (FINDING 25), so the entry window this file
//   permits is 20-40x wider than the one the source demonstrates.
//   And there is no end-of-day flat anywhere in v1-v7: a break accepted at 15:55 opens a trade the
//   simulator then carries through the overnight and into later sessions, with `hitT`/`hitS`
//   evaluated on out-of-session bars and `tradesToday` resetting underneath it — on a system whose
//   own module 5 opens with "the longer you're in the market the more they're going to take
//   advantage of you... we need to get in and we need to get out" [00:18]/[00:27].
//   Both are now inputs, both OFF, with a dashboard row and counters that say how much each WOULD
//   bind. Retuning a window nobody has measured is the failure this project exists to avoid.
//
// ⚠ v7 ADDS NO RULE AND CHANGES NO DEFAULT. Its signal set and trade record are identical to v6's.
//   It measures one thing the corpus says should be alarming: HOW LONG A TRADE LASTS RELATIVE TO
//   THE BAR IT IS SIMULATED ON. In all five NY live streams the target ladder is traversed in
//   seconds-to-minutes (FINDING 23) — 1R→4R in 3m11s in `video1270175432`, inside the FIRST 5m bar
//   of the session — while this file's own universe is 5m and 15m. If a trade lives and dies inside
//   its entry bar, no bar-close simulator can resolve it: the stop/target ordering is unknowable and
//   the "ambiguous bar books the stop" convention (v2 fix 4) decides the trade record instead of the
//   market. The new dashboard row and plots make that measurable on one chart instead of argued.
//
// ⚠ v6 IS THE FIRST VERSION SINCE v2 WHOSE DEFAULT SIGNAL SET DIFFERS FROM ITS PREDECESSOR'S.
//   v3, v4 and v5 all shipped their corrections behind switches that default to the old behaviour.
//   v6's structure-invalidation rule ships ON, because it is a rule the source STATES rather than
//   an interpretation of one it leaves open — the same treatment v2 gave the touch counter. Set
//   "Structure invalidation" to "Confirmed pivots only (v1-v5)" to reproduce v5 exactly.
//
// NOT ONE NUMBER IN THIS FILE HAS EVER COME FROM A RUN. This workstream has no runId. Every
// default here is either a source quote or a labelled interpretation; nothing is a measurement.
//
// A faithful, mechanical rendering of MAMBA's system — the New York / NQ+YM trader.
// See legacy-forex/SYSTEM.md for every rule with its verbatim quote and timestamp.
//
// ⚠ THE COMMITTED TRANSCRIPT CORPUS IS TWO TRADERS, NOT ONE (SYSTEM.md FINDING 6, 2026-09-05).
//   Eight numbered modules + five NY live streams are MAMBA — NQ/YM, New York, 5m/15m. That is
//   this file. Four "videoNNNN" live streams + module 4 are COACH LUCA — gold (MGC), Asia session,
//   1m-1h, ATR stops, fair value gaps, a 200 MA. NOTHING from Luca belongs in this indicator.
//   (Module 4 is the one exception this file draws on, and only where Luca is quoting Mamba's own
//   numbers back — flagged at the point of use.)
//
// INDICATOR, not a strategy — it takes no positions. It draws every element of his method and a
// dashboard that names the exact condition currently blocking a trade.
//
// RUN IT ON: NQ or YM (or NAS100 / US30), 5-minute or 15-minute chart. Nothing else — he is
// emphatic that those two instruments and those two timeframes are the entire universe:
//   "NQ and YM is the only thing we trade. We trade nothing else."      (3._WHAT_TO_TRADE 01:45)
//   "15 and 5 is the only place we're ever going to be."                (5._ANALYZING_TIME_FRAMES 03:18)
//
// ── HIS STACK, IN ORDER ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   1. SESSION    New York only, first entry 09:30 ET      (8. 00:14 / 00:44)
//   2. STRUCTURE  HH+HL = longs only. LL+LH = shorts only.
//                 Anything else is consolidation = NO TRADE (6. 01:53 / 06:22)
//   3. LEVEL      support = floor, resistance = ceiling, validated by repeated DISTINCT touches;
//                 a broken level flips role                 (7. 00:27 / 00:47 / 01:51)
//   4. BREAK      price breaks the level in the structure's direction. The retest is a BONUS,
//                 not a gate: "we don't need it to come back and retest"  (7. 05:35)
//   5. VOLUME     must be present. "If there's no volume, there's no trading."  (9. 07:51)
//   6. STOP       just beyond the broken level, never the far one — AND capped: a structural stop
//                 wider than his per-instrument maximum REJECTS the setup — "64 points, my
//                 account's gone if I do that"              (11. 00:07 / 01:26)
//   7. TARGET     NOT a fixed 1:3-1:5. It is the mean achieved R of his last ~6 closed trades,
//                 losses scored 0, rounded, recomputed daily — "we are averaging a one, two,
//                 three... which means we are going to go for one, two, threes"  (10. 00:38-05:43)
//                 1:3-1:5 (9. 05:53) is where that output usually LANDS, not the rule.
//   8. MANAGE     break-even at +1R (+1.5R at the latest) is the only part he states as
//                 non-negotiable; what happens ABOVE that is under-determined in the source —
//                 see the TRAIL note below. (11. 02:13 / 03:59 / 06:38 / 08:02 / 09:01)
//   9. LIMIT      max 2 trades/day, 3 exceptionally — and the cap counts ACROSS NQ+YM together,
//                 not per instrument (video1263885792 03:58). Realised rate is ~0.6-0.8 per
//                 session, about a third of the cap (10. 00:38 / 02:22).
//
// ── THE TRAIL IS UNDER-DETERMINED IN THE SOURCE — v2 STOPS PRETENDING OTHERWISE ────────────────
// Module 11 supports THREE different trail rules, in the same nine minutes, and v1 silently
// hard-coded one of them as if it were the rule:
//   (a) STEP EVERY +1R — "Every time this thing moves up in the same distance as our stop loss,
//       which is 25 points, we're going to adjust our stop loss" [02:13]; "as price starts to
//       push up even higher, you just adjust with it" [03:59].
//   (b) FREEZE AT +1R — what his own worked example actually does: at 1:5 he moves the stop to
//       1:1 and leaves it. "We're now at a one to five... we're going to put that down to a one
//       to one" [06:27-06:38].
//   (c) BREAK-EVEN ONLY — offered explicitly as a complete alternative: "or don't adjust your
//       stops at all" [09:01], with the floor being "at worst, at the least, please put your
//       stops to break even at a one to one point five at the worst" [08:02-08:19].
// Only the break-even floor is stated as non-negotiable. The mode is now an INPUT, defaulting to
// (b) because that is the one he demonstrates rather than describes, and the dashboard names the
// live mode. This is a source ambiguity, not a modelling choice — do not resolve it by taste.
//
// ── ONE CONTRADICTION, DRAWN RATHER THAN RESOLVED ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// The modules gate direction on structure (bullish -> buys only). The live streams show him
// pre-marking a break level on BOTH sides of BOTH instruments and taking whichever goes:
//   "Be prepared for all four positions on the screen."     (video1263885792 01:28)
// The course gate is left ON by default. "Show counter-structure bracket" draws the level he would
// have marked but this gate forbids, as a dotted line, so the disagreement is visible on the chart.
//
// ── DELIBERATELY NOT DRAWN ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// No higher-timeframe bias filter. That is not an omission — he rules it out explicitly:
//   "There's no overall bias. We're not seeing what the daily is doing."   (5. 03:00)
// This is the opposite of the War Formation and 3M systems, and the three must not be mixed.
//
// ── WHAT v2 CHANGED, AND WHY (SYSTEM.md FINDING 17) ───────────────────────────────────────────
//  1. TOUCH COUNTING WAS COUNTING BARS, NOT TOUCHES, AND COUNTED THE LEVEL'S OWN PIVOT WINDOW.
//     v1 incremented once per BAR whose high or low sat within tolerance of the level, scanning
//     all 200 lookback bars including the pivot's own formation neighbourhood. The pivot bar
//     scores a touch by construction (|high - lvl| = 0), and each of the 2*pivLen neighbours
//     scores one whenever the local swing spans less than the tolerance. So "3 touches" could be
//     — and on a quiet swing usually was — satisfied the instant the pivot confirmed, with ZERO
//     revisits. His own definition is distinct visits: "one touch two touch three four five six
//     seven touches with those wicks" (7. 00:47). v2 counts distinct band ENTRIES and excludes
//     the pivot's own +/- pivLen window. BOTH counts are shown side by side so the size of the
//     correction is visible on a live chart instead of argued about.
//     ⚠ THIS MAY TAKE THE SIGNAL COUNT TO ZERO. That is HARD LESSON 8's tell and it is expected
//     to be checked, not assumed either way. `minTouch` is NOT retuned here — retuning it without
//     a measurement would be exactly the fabrication this project forbids.
//  2. THE ROLE FLIP WAS ADVERTISED IN THE HEADER AND ABSENT FROM THE CODE. "Support broke and
//     then became resistance... they turn into each other and they do very often" (7. 01:51-02:19),
//     "a break and a retest of support into resistance" (7. 07:14). v2 tracks and draws flipped
//     levels. It does NOT let them fire signals by default — that would change the signal set and
//     the audit at the same time.
//  3. INTRABAR SEQUENCING. v1 moved the stop using THIS bar's close and then tested THIS bar's
//     low against the moved stop, so a bar that closed at +2R could book a "+1R trail" exit on a
//     low that happened before the run-up. v2 manages on the PREVIOUS bar's R.
//  4. AN AMBIGUOUS BAR (target and stop both touched) BOOKED THE WIN. Now books the stop, the
//     conservative convention. It matters because achieved R feeds the rolling-mean target.
//  5. ONE MAX-STOP FOR TWO INSTRUMENTS. "Mamba uses 20 points on NASDAQ, let's say 30 points on
//     YM" (4. 04:09 — Luca quoting Mamba). 30 points is ~0.14% of NQ and ~0.07% of YM: the same
//     number is a materially different gate. v2 resolves per instrument and shows which is live.
//
// ── WHAT v3 CHANGED, AND WHY (tick #9 — SYSTEM.md FINDING 18) ─────────────────────────────────
// v2 fixed the max-stop CAP per instrument and left the PADDING in different units from the cap.
// The padding is a percentage of price; the cap is absolute index points. At the price levels he
// is actually trading — both of which are read off his own screen in the committed transcripts —
// the padding alone eats most of the entire risk budget before any structural distance counts:
//
//   NQ 24,954.50 (4. 03:06)   pad 0.05% = 12.48 pts = 62.4% of the 20-pt cap  -> 7.5 pts left
//   YM 46,942    (4. 07:23)   pad 0.05% = 23.47 pts = 78.2% of the 30-pt cap  -> 6.5 pts left
//
// So `useMaxStop` (ON by default) was rejecting any break whose close sat more than ~7 points
// beyond the level it had just broken. And because the pad SCALES WITH PRICE while the cap does
// not, the gate becomes mathematically unsatisfiable — pad alone >= cap, so no setup can pass at
// any distance — at NQ 40,000 and YM 60,000. The indicator had a built-in expiry date.
//
//  6. PADDING IS NOW IN HIS UNITS, NOT PERCENT. Nothing in the source states a padding distance:
//     "our stop loss is below where it would come back to retest" (7. 05:43), "just needs to
//     simply start just below that support" (11. 00:15). The pad is the word "just", and at NQ it
//     was consuming half of the 25-point stop that whole sentence describes. The one padding
//     quantity the source DOES define is the tick — 0.25 pts on NQ, 1 pt on YM (4. 02:06 / 07:07)
//     — so the default is now one tick (1.0% and 3.3% of the respective caps). This is the
//     minimal source-expressible reading of "just below", labelled as an interpretation, not a
//     stated rule. The % mode is retained so v1/v2 behaviour stays reproducible for diffing,
//     exactly as `touchMode` retains v1's counting.
//  7. THE NQ CAP DEFAULT FOLLOWED THE WEAKER SOURCE. 20 pts comes from module 4 [04:09], which is
//     LUCA DESCRIBING what Mamba uses. Module 11 is Mamba DEMONSTRATING: "That's a 25 point stop
//     loss, which is solid. That's actually a really good number" [00:20-00:23], "we have a 25
//     point stop" [01:43] — and a 20-pt stop at [06:50]. A 20-pt cap therefore REJECTS the exact
//     worked example module 11 teaches. This file already set a precedent for this exact kind of
//     ambiguity in v2's trail-mode fix — default to the one he DEMONSTRATES rather than describes
//     — so the NQ cap default moves 20 -> 25. The source brackets it at 20-25 and refuses 64
//     ("my account's gone", 11. 01:26); it does not pin it, and the input is where that lives.
//     YM's 30 is unchanged and remains SINGLE-SOURCED from Luca — module 11 has no YM example.
//  8. THE PAD FORMULA WAS WRITTEN TWICE. The projection used `padNow` and the trade-open block
//     recomputed the same expression inline, so any change to one silently diverged from the
//     other. One definition now.
//
// ── WHAT v4 CHANGED, AND WHY (tick #10 — SYSTEM.md FINDING 19) ────────────────────────────────
// v3 spent a whole tick getting the stop PADDING into the cap's units. It never asked what units
// the TOUCH TOLERANCE was in. Same defect class, one order of magnitude larger, and it had been
// sitting in the file since v1:
//
//   NQ 24,954.50 (4. 03:06)   tol 0.10% = 24.95 pts  → band 49.9 pts = 200% of the 25-pt cap
//   YM 46,942    (4. 07:23)   tol 0.10% = 46.94 pts  → band 93.9 pts = 313% of the 30-pt cap
//
// THE CONSEQUENCE, AND IT IS AN OUTRIGHT CONTRADICTION BETWEEN TWO GATES:
//   stop budget = cap − pad = 24.75 pts (NQ) / 29.0 pts (YM) — the most a break may sit beyond
//   the level and still be accepted by the stop-width gate. The touch tolerance is 24.95 / 46.94.
//   tol > budget on BOTH instruments. So EVERY break this system is capable of trading lies
//   inside the band its own level definition still calls "touching" — "price can't break it,
//   it's stuck" (7. 00:47). One gate calls the bar a touch; the other calls it a break; they are
//   describing the same bar and the same level. The tolerance is also 100× the pad on NQ and 47×
//   on YM: v3 tuned the level's padding to a quarter of a point while the level's own identity
//   was fuzzy to twenty-five.
//
//   9. TOLERANCE IS NOW A FRACTION OF THE LIVE MAX STOP, NOT A % OF PRICE. The source states no
//      width. It states that the level IS a zone — "support zones" (7. 01:21), "you make this
//      little thicker because it's the whole thing" (7. 04:35) — and never how thick. So the
//      number is an interpretation either way, and the only defensible constraint available is
//      COMMENSURABILITY: a level's own width must be smaller than the stop that has to sit
//      outside it, or "just below that support" (11. 00:15) is undefined. Anchoring tolerance to
//      the cap enforces that by construction and removes the price-scaling drift for free. The
//      default 0.20 (5 pts NQ, 6 pts YM) is an INTERPRETATION, exposed as an input and
//      pre-registered as a one-dimensional test — it is NOT a measured optimum.
//      Why not ATR, the obvious volatility-relative fix: ATR stops are COACH LUCA's, and nothing
//      from Luca belongs in this indicator (see the corpus warning at the top).
//      % mode is retained and reproduces v1-v3 EXACTLY, including its own minor defect — it sizes
//      the band off the CURRENT bar's `close` rather than off the level, so a fixed level's touch
//      count can change on a bar where nothing happened near it. Kept for diffing, not for use.
//  10. THE BREAK MAY NOW BE REQUIRED TO CLEAR THE BAND. OFF by default: turning it on changes the
//      signal set, and a correction tick does not audit and alter what fires at the same time
//      (v2's precedent with `flipTrades`). Under v1-v3 tolerance this option was not merely off,
//      it was UNSATISFIABLE — clearing a 24.95-pt band leaves −0.2 pts of stop budget on NQ. At
//      v4's tolerance it leaves 19.75, so it becomes a real pre-registered test for the first time.
//  11. INSTRUMENT RESOLUTION MOVED ABOVE THE LEVEL BLOCK, because the tolerance now depends on it.
//      No behaviour change: it reads only inputs and `syminfo`.
//
// ⚠ WHAT v4 DOES **NOT** CLAIM: the DIRECTION of the bias on the touch count. A too-wide band
//   makes each bar easier to score as a touch, and simultaneously makes distinct VISITS harder to
//   separate — price must leave a 50-pt band entirely before a new visit can begin. The two push
//   opposite ways and no arithmetic settles which dominates. That is a chart measurement, and the
//   dashboard row + data-window plots added here exist so it can be made instead of argued.
//
// ── WHAT v5 CHANGED, AND WHY (tick #11 — SYSTEM.md FINDING 20) ─────────────────────────────────
// Ticks #9 and #10 each found a gate whose UNITS were wrong. The volume gate's defect is a
// different one: the gate's THRESHOLD (volMult 1.0) is fine, and the quantity underneath it is
// not the quantity the source uses at all.
//
//  12. `volLen = 20` IS A BAR COUNT ON A SYSTEM THAT TRADES TWO TIMEFRAMES, SO IT IS TWO
//      DIFFERENT GATES. 20 bars is 100 minutes on 5m and 5 HOURS on 15m. The NY session he trades
//      is 09:30-16:00 ET = 390 min = 78 bars on 5m, 26 on 15m. So the trailing baseline does not
//      consist purely of session bars until:
//         5m  — 11:10 ET, i.e. the first 20 of 78 session bars (26%) are judged against a
//               baseline reaching back to 07:50 ET;
//         15m — 14:30 ET, i.e. the first 20 of 26 session bars (77%) are judged against a
//               baseline reaching back to 04:30 ET, entirely overnight.
//      On 15m more than three-quarters of the session he trades is scored against an overnight
//      average. This is the ledger's HARD LESSON 10 shape — a flat parameter binding differently
//      per timeframe — in a file whose own header insists 5m and 15m are the entire universe.
//      DIRECTION IS SIGNED HERE, unlike v4's tolerance: overnight index-futures volume is
//      structurally below cash-session volume, so a mostly-overnight baseline makes
//      `volume > avg` EASIER. The gate is LOOSEST at 09:30 — the moment his own course says the
//      volume is best anyway ("you're gonna have the most amount of volume", 8. 02:05) and the
//      moment FINDING 9 says he takes most of his ~0.7-0.9 trades — and only starts binding in
//      the afternoon, when he has usually finished. It filters where he does not trade.
//      MAGNITUDE IS UNMEASURED and is not asserted anywhere. A `Vol baseline` dashboard row now
//      shows the baseline's span in minutes and how many of its bars are outside the session, so
//      one live chart settles it. A `Session-to-date average` baseline is added as a
//      pre-registered option and is OFF by default — the default baseline is unchanged, so with
//      default inputs v5's signal set and trade record are IDENTICAL to v4's.
//  13. THE QUANTITY IS NOT HIS. He defines volume kinematically — "volume is how fast is the
//      markets moving" (9. 00:41) — and every one of his three worked diagnoses is read off
//      PRICE DISPLACEMENT, never off a histogram: "moving sideways, which means we're moving left
//      to right" (02:30), "we're not going left to right, we are going straight up like price is
//      supposed to" (06:18-06:22), "a little bit sideways... not the best volume" (09:24-09:31).
//      He never reads a volume bar aloud, never states a threshold, and never names a lookback.
//      `volume > sma(volume, 20)` is therefore an INTERPRETATION with no source support — the
//      only gate in this file whose underlying quantity is not the one the source uses. It is
//      left in place (there is nothing measured to replace it with) but it is now labelled.
//  14. AND IT IS IN THE WRONG PLACE. All three diagnoses are made on bars AFTER the break —
//      "if price breaks OUT and slowly starts to consolidate" (01:03); the 17:30 example is judged
//      after the short is already open; "boom that is a good volume" (06:18) is after the long is
//      taken. His PRE-entry proxy for volume is not a histogram at all, it is the SESSION: "why
//      did we have volume? because we traded during New York session like we're supposed to"
//      (06:31-06:37). v1-v4 have it exactly backwards — a hard pre-entry gate and NO post-entry
//      test. That also makes the pre-entry gate partly REDUNDANT with the session gate already in
//      the file, which is War Formation E82's shape; recorded as a pre-registered ablation, not
//      claimed.
//  15. TWO STATED RULES WERE IN SYSTEM.md's MECHANICAL SPEC AND ABSENT FROM THE CODE — the same
//      defect class as v2's role flip.
//      (a) THE EARLY EXIT. "No volume doesn't look good. We're out of this trade. Yes, we're
//          negative right now, but we would rather lose this small amount, about SEVEN POINTS,
//          than let it go all the way up and take us out for 15 points, more than double that"
//          (9. 03:22-03:38). Now implemented, OFF by default (it changes the trade record).
//      (b) CONVICTION SIZING. "The volume is okay, the setup looks good but not great. I'm gonna
//          risk less. Not a tighter stop loss — my contract size is going to get smaller"
//          (9. 09:45-09:58). A three-state rule against a binary gate. This is an INDICATOR and
//          takes no positions, so it is rendered as a dashboard row only, and the band edge that
//          separates "okay" from "good" is UNSOURCED and printed in the cell so it reads as a
//          knob, not a finding. It never touches `volOK` and never changes a signal.
//
// ⚠ WHAT v5 DOES **NOT** CLAIM. No magnitude. Whether the overnight baseline changes the gate's
//   hit rate by a lot or a little is a chart measurement and is instrumented, not asserted. The
//   `volDeadBars` default is UNSOURCED — the source states the behaviour and its outcome, never a
//   bar count — which is why the exit ships OFF. And 15 points (9. 03:38) is a THIRD stop-width
//   figure in this corpus alongside module 11's 25 and 20; the instrument is not identified in
//   that passage and the trade is one he is holding up as a mistake, so the caps are NOT retuned
//   on it. It is recorded in FINDING 20 as an observation.
//
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v6 — `pivLen` WAS THE LAST UN-AUDITED NUMBER, AND THE DEFECT IS NOT IN THE NUMBER (FINDING 21)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  16. STRUCTURE INVALIDATION IS A PRICE EVENT IN THE SOURCE AND A CONFIRMED-PIVOT EVENT IN THE
//      CODE. He kills a structure the instant price trades through the swing it is built on:
//      "You see this level here. We did not break past the previous spot. So that previous higher
//      high and higher low we didn't break past that" (6. 03:30-03:35); "now if price would have
//      came down here and started to push down in this way BOOM that is now a lower low" (6.
//      03:41). v1-v5 had no invalidation rule of their own — `bullStruct` simply stayed true until
//      a NEW pivot confirmed and changed one of the four comparisons. `ta.pivotlow(low, 5, 5)`
//      cannot confirm until pivLen bars after the swing low, so the gate stayed bullish for AT
//      LEAST pivLen bars past the break — 25 min on 5m, 75 min on 15m — plus the entire length of
//      the down leg, and permitted longs the whole way. v6 adds the price rule as the default,
//      with "Confirmed pivots only" retained to reproduce v1-v5. IT MAY TAKE THE SIGNAL SET TO
//      ZERO in chop; that is written down here BEFORE any chart is loaded, and the "Struct guard"
//      dashboard row names it as the blocker if it does. HARD LESSON 8's check was run first: the
//      arming event (a confirmed pivot low) guarantees the confirming bar's close sits ABOVE that
//      low, so the latch cannot be killed by the price action that arms it.
//  17. `pivLen` IS NOT A ONE-DIMENSIONAL KNOB, AND THE PRE-REGISTERED TEST LIST SAID IT WAS. It
//      appears in three places: the structure gate (ta.pivothigh/low), the TRADED LEVELS themselves
//      (`resLvl = lastPH`), and the ±pivLen exclusion window inside the touch counter. Changing it
//      moves the direction gate, the price of every level, and the level-validation test at once.
//      Any sweep of it is a three-parameter change and must be reported as one.
//  18. THE CODE CANNOT BUILD A STRUCTURE STATE INSIDE A 15m NEW YORK SESSION. It needs two pivot
//      highs AND two pivot lows. Two same-side pivots must sit at least pivLen+1 bars apart, and
//      the outer two each need pivLen bars of confirmation, so the floor is
//      pivLen + (pivLen+1) + pivLen + 1 = 17 bars at pivLen 5; a realistic alternating H-L-H-L
//      sequence with half-swings of pivLen+1 is ~29. The NY session is 390 min = 26 bars on 15m
//      and 78 on 5m. So on 15m the state is ALWAYS inherited from bars before 09:30, and on 5m it
//      is for the first fifth to third of the session. Whether that is wrong is not resolvable
//      from the source — he reads structure off a chart that shows overnight bars — so v6
//      INSTRUMENTS it ("Struct age": the age of the oldest defining pivot and how many of the four
//      formed pre-open) and asserts nothing. There is no recency bound on those pivots anywhere in
//      the file, and the source states none either.
//  19. WHAT IS CLEAN, RECORDED BECAUSE A CLEAN GATE IS ALSO A RESULT. `bullStruct = hh AND hl` is
//      exactly "higher highs followed by higher lows" (6. 01:53). `consolidating` as the residual
//      lumps a broadening range (higher high + lower low) in with the sideways one he actually
//      describes (6. 06:04), but both are correctly excluded from trading, so the label is loose
//      and the gate outcome is right. `pivLen = 5` itself is UNSOURCED — he draws swings by eye —
//      and is NOT retuned here, for the reason in item 17.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v8 — THE SESSION END WAS NEVER SOURCED, AND NOTHING EVER CLOSED THE DAY (tick #15, FINDING 25)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  20. THE ENTRY WINDOW IS 390 MINUTES AND THE CORPUS SUPPORTS ~20. Module 8 is the session module
//      and it states only a start. Every one of the five NY live streams is over within minutes of
//      that start, and each closes with day-end language, not stream-end language:
//        video1270175432 — open anchored at [02:03] by two independent countdowns; stream ends
//                          [08:12] → the WHOLE broadcast day is 6m09s of post-open time.
//        video1855004398 — "hiccups at fucking 6 30 am" [02:21] anchors the open; last rung
//                          "nice target three" [09:34], ends [09:56] → ~7m35s post-open.
//        video1038794732 — "target two for us 30 target for NASDAQ and THAT IS HOW WE'RE GONNA BE
//                          ENDING THE DAY" [08:37], of a 534s file.
//        video1263885792 — "we'll come back tomorrow" [06:44], of a 427s file.
//        video1979454677 — the longest at 1149s; its last rung is [12:11] and it signs off "big
//                          money making day, we'll come back tomorrow" [18:50].
//      On his two timeframes 390 minutes is 78 bars (5m) and 26 (15m). ~20 minutes is 4 and 1.
//      `entryCut` exposes that as a switch; it is OFF because the source states no end, and the
//      dashboard's "Entry window" row counts how many entries fall outside it either way.
//  21. AND THIS SHARPENS FINDING 21 RATHER THAN REPEATING IT. v6 measured that the code needs ≥17
//      bars (pivLen 5) and realistically ~29 before ANY structure state can exist, and concluded
//      the state is always inherited from pre-open bars on 15m and for "the first fifth to third"
//      of the session on 5m. Under a 20-minute entry window that softer half hardens: 4 bars on 5m
//      against a 17-bar floor means the direction gate at EVERY legal entry is built ENTIRELY from
//      bars outside the session he trades — on BOTH timeframes, not just 15m. The "Struct age" row
//      already prints this ("n of 4 formed BEFORE today's open"); v8 only makes it inevitable
//      rather than likely.
//  22. NOTHING EVER CLOSED THE DAY. v1-v7 open a trade and hold it until target, stop, or the v5
//      volume-death cut. None of those is time-bounded, and `inSess` gates ENTRY only, so an open
//      trade survives the close, prices its stop and target against overnight bars, and can still
//      be live when `newDay` zeroes `tradesToday` beneath it. Against module 5's opening premise
//      that is not a small deviation, and it is not cosmetic either: whatever R such a trade
//      eventually books is pushed into the rolling-mean window that sets his TARGET (10. 02:56),
//      so an overnight-resolved trade he would never have held rewrites tomorrow's target.
//      `flatEOD` closes at the last in-session close. OFF by default, and evaluated BEFORE the
//      stop/target block because that close chronologically precedes the following bar's range.
//  23. WHAT IS CLEAN HERE, CHECKED NOT ASSUMED. `newDay = ta.change(time("1D")) != 0` was the other
//      suspect and it is fine on both instrument families: a CME daily bar rolls at the Globex
//      open (17:00/18:00 ET) and a cash-index daily bar at midnight, and BOTH sit outside
//      09:30-16:00, so exactly one New York session falls inside each "day" and neither the
//      2-trade cap nor the v5 session-to-date volume accumulator can be split across one.
//
// ⚠ WHAT v8 DOES **NOT** CLAIM. Not that his window IS 20 minutes. Five broadcast days are a
//   sample he selected, and they are selected toward days that resolved fast — a day with no setup
//   at 09:40 is exactly the day not worth streaming. What the corpus supports is that 390 minutes
//   is unevidenced and 1600 is not his number; where the true cutoff sits is unmeasured, which is
//   why `entryCutMins` is an input with a counter next to it and not a new default.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

indicator("Legacy Forex Trader — Complete Visualiser", overlay=true, max_lines_count=500, max_labels_count=500, max_boxes_count=500)

// ── INPUTS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gS = "Session"
useSession = input.bool(true,  "New York session only",            group=gS)
sessTime   = input.session("0930-1600", "Session (ET)",            group=gS,
     tooltip="v8 audit (FINDING 25): the START is stated four ways in module 8 ('930 a.m. Eastern' [00:44], '6 30 a.m. Pacific' [00:59], 'be on 20 minutes early... you get on at 6 10' [01:03-01:16]). The END is stated NOWHERE in the corpus. 1600 is the RTH close — a fact about the exchange, not one of his rules — and it has been in this file unexamined since v1. It makes the legal entry window 390 minutes = 78 bars on 5m and 26 on 15m, against five recorded days that are each over within ~20 minutes of the open. Do not read 1600 as sourced.")
sessTZ     = input.string("America/New_York", "Timezone",          group=gS)
entryCut   = input.bool(false, "Entry cutoff: N minutes after the open", group=gS,
     tooltip="v8, FINDING 25. OFF by default because the source states no cutoff — only that every day it records is finished within minutes of the open. Turning it ON changes the signal set, which a correction tick does not do at the same time as auditing (the treatment v2 gave flipTrades, v4 gave breakClears and v5 gave the session-to-date baseline). The 'Entry window' dashboard row counts entries beyond the cutoff whether it is on or off, so one live chart says how much it would bind.")
entryCutMins = input.int(20, "Entry cutoff (minutes after the open)", group=gS, minval=1,
     tooltip="UNSOURCED as a rule. 20 is the longest post-open interval any activity is recorded at in the whole corpus (video1979454677's second trade reaches target two at [12:11] of a stream whose open is un-anchored). It is a reading of five days HE selected to broadcast, biased toward days that resolved fast, and it is NOT a measured optimum.")
flatEOD    = input.bool(false, "Flatten any open trade at the session close", group=gS,
     tooltip="v8, FINDING 25. v1-v7 had NO time-based exit at all: a trade opened at 15:55 is carried through the overnight and into later sessions, priced against out-of-session bars, while `tradesToday` resets underneath it — and whatever R it eventually books is pushed into the rolling-mean window that sets tomorrow's TARGET (10. 02:56). Module 5 opens on the opposite premise: 'the longer you're in the market the more they're going to take advantage of you... we need to get in and we need to get out' [00:18]/[00:27]. OFF by default because it changes the trade record; the 'Hold window' row counts overnight holds either way.")

gStruct = "Market structure"
pivLen   = input.int(5,  "Swing pivot strength (bars each side)",  group=gStruct, minval=1,
     tooltip="UNSOURCED — he draws swings by eye and states no bar count. v6 audit (FINDING 21): this is NOT a one-dimensional knob. pivLen sets (a) the structure gate, (b) the traded levels themselves (resLvl = the last pivot high), and (c) the ±pivLen exclusion window in the touch counter. Sweeping it moves all three at once, so it must not be pre-registered as a single-parameter test. Arithmetic that binds it: the code needs at least 2*pivLen + (pivLen+1) + 1 bars before ANY structure state can exist — 17 bars at pivLen 5, ~29 for a realistic four-swing sequence — against a 26-bar 15m New York session. Read the 'Struct age' dashboard row before changing it.")
structInval = input.string("Price breaks the defining swing (6. 03:35)", "Structure invalidation",
     options=["Price breaks the defining swing (6. 03:35)","Confirmed pivots only (v1-v5)"], group=gStruct,
     tooltip="v6, FINDING 21 — the headline defect. The source kills a structure on a PRICE event: 'that previous higher high and higher low we didn't break past that... now if price would have came down here and started to push down in this way boom that is now a lower low' (6. 03:35-03:41). v1-v5 killed it on a CONFIRMED PIVOT event, which ta.pivotlow cannot deliver until pivLen bars AFTER the swing low — 25 min on 5m, 75 min on 15m, plus the length of the down leg. In that gap the direction gate still permitted trades in the direction price had already broken. This mode closes it: bullish structure dies the moment a close prints below the higher low it is built on, and re-arms only on a fresh confirmed pivot low. IT MAY TAKE THE SIGNAL SET TO ZERO in chop — that is written down BEFORE anyone runs it, and the 'Struct guard' dashboard row names it as the blocker if it does. 'Confirmed pivots only' reproduces v1-v5 exactly, for diffing.")

gLvl = "Support / resistance"
tolMode  = input.string("Fraction of the live max stop (v4 default)", "Touch tolerance unit", options=["Fraction of the live max stop (v4 default)","% of price (v1-v3 behaviour)"], group=gLvl,
     tooltip="v4: the source never states a zone width, only that a level IS a zone (7. 01:21 / 04:35). In % mode the band was ±24.95 pts on NQ and ±46.94 on YM — wider than the entire stop budget, so every tradeable break was still inside the band the counter calls a touch. Anchoring to the cap makes the two commensurable by construction. % mode reproduces v1-v3 exactly (including its close-anchoring defect) and is here for diffing, not for use.")
tolFrac  = input.float(0.20, "Touch tolerance, fraction of the live max stop", group=gLvl, minval=0.01, maxval=1.0,
     tooltip="0.20 → ±5 pts on NQ (cap 25), ±6 on YM (cap 30). This is an INTERPRETATION — the source gives no width — exposed as an input and pre-registered as a one-dimensional test. It is NOT a measured optimum and must not be quoted as one. The only thing arithmetic pins is the ceiling: tolerance must stay below the stop budget (cap − pad).")
touchTol = input.float(0.10, "Touch tolerance, % of price (v1-v3 behaviour only)", group=gLvl,
     tooltip="Legacy. 0.10% of price = 24.95 pts at NQ 24,954.50 and 46.94 at YM 46,942 — see SYSTEM.md FINDING 19.")
breakClears = input.bool(false, "Break must clear the tolerance band", group=gLvl,
     tooltip="OFF by default on purpose. v1-v4 score a break on a close one tick past the pivot, while the counter still calls that bar a touch of the same level — the two gates contradict each other. Turning this on resolves it in the source's favour ('price can't break it', 7. 00:47) but CHANGES THE SIGNAL SET, so it is a pre-registered test, not a fix applied here. Under v1-v3 tolerance it was unsatisfiable outright.")
minTouch = input.int(3,      "Touches to validate a level",        group=gLvl, minval=2,
     tooltip="v2: this now gates on DISTINCT visits, not bars-in-band. It is a much stricter test than v1's. NOT retuned — the correct value is a measurement nobody has made yet. v4 audit: 3 is the one level-gate number the source DOES support — 'we have all these touches one two three is resistance' (7. 04:31), against 7 (00:47), 4 (01:04) and 8 (01:41) elsewhere. It is a floor he shows, not a threshold he states.")
touchMode= input.string("Distinct visits (v2, his definition)", "Touch counting", options=["Distinct visits (v2, his definition)","Bars in band (v1 behaviour)"], group=gLvl,
     tooltip="Kept switchable so v1's behaviour is reproducible and the two can be compared on one chart. 'Bars in band' is known wrong; it is here for diffing, not for use.")
showFlip = input.bool(true,  "Draw flipped levels (broken support -> resistance)", group=gLvl)
flipTrades=input.bool(false, "Allow flipped levels to fire signals", group=gLvl,
     tooltip="OFF by default on purpose: turning it on changes the signal set, and v2's job was to correct the level test, not to alter what fires.")

gVol = "Volume"
useVol   = input.bool(true, "Require volume",                      group=gVol,
     tooltip="v5 audit: the source never mechanises volume as a histogram test. He defines it kinematically — 'volume is how fast is the markets moving' (9. 00:41) — and reads it off price displacement every time. This gate is an INTERPRETATION, not a stated rule. See SYSTEM.md FINDING 20.")
volLen   = input.int(20,    "Volume average length (bars)",        group=gVol,
     tooltip="UNSOURCED — the source names no lookback. And it is a BAR count on a system that trades two timeframes: 20 bars = 100 min on 5m but 5 HOURS on 15m. At the 09:30 open the window reaches back to 07:50 (5m) / 04:30 (15m), so the baseline is partly overnight for the first 20 of 78 session bars on 5m and 20 of 26 on 15m. Read the 'Vol baseline' dashboard row on a live chart before changing this.")
volMult  = input.float(1.0, "Volume must exceed baseline ×",       group=gVol)
volBaseline= input.string("Trailing N bars (v1-v4 behaviour)", "Volume baseline", options=["Trailing N bars (v1-v4 behaviour)","Session-to-date average (v5 option)"], group=gVol,
     tooltip="v5: the trailing baseline is contaminated by overnight bars for most of the 15m session (see volLen). A session-to-date mean removes that and makes the 5m and 15m gates commensurable. It is OFF by default because switching it CHANGES THE SIGNAL SET, and a correction tick does not audit and alter at the same time — the same treatment v2 gave flipTrades and v4 gave breakClears. Pre-registered as a one-dimensional test.")
volGoodMult= input.float(1.5, "Full-size conviction threshold, × baseline", group=gVol,
     tooltip="DISPLAY ONLY — never touches volOK, never changes a signal. Renders the three-state sizing rule at 9. 09:45-09:58 ('the volume is okay... I'm gonna risk less... my contract size is going to get smaller') which v1-v4 had no representation of at all. The band edge between 'okay' and 'good' is UNSOURCED; it is printed in the dashboard cell so it reads as a knob and not as a finding.")
volDeadExit= input.bool(false, "Exit early when volume dies after entry (9. 03:22)", group=gVol,
     tooltip="OFF by default: this CHANGES THE TRADE RECORD. 'No volume doesn't look good. We're out of this trade... we would rather lose this small amount, about seven points, than let it go all the way up and take us out for 15 points' (9. 03:22-03:38). The behaviour is stated plainly and was missing from v1-v4 entirely; the bar count below is not stated anywhere, which is why this ships off.")
volDeadBars= input.int(3,   "…after this many consecutive no-volume bars", group=gVol, minval=1,
     tooltip="UNSOURCED. The source states the behaviour and its outcome (7 pts cut vs a 15-pt stop) and never a bar count. This is a placeholder exposed as a pre-registered test, not a measured value, and must never be quoted as one.")
volDeadR   = input.float(0.0, "…and only while the trade is below this R", group=gVol,
     tooltip="0.0 is the minimal source-expressible reading of 'price is not moving down… it's moving sideways' (9. 02:25-02:35): the trade has not advanced at all. Same style of minimal reading as v3's one-tick pad.")

gRisk = "Risk"
padMode   = input.string("Ticks beyond the level (v3 default)", "Stop padding unit", options=["Ticks beyond the level (v3 default)","% of price (v1/v2 behaviour)"], group=gRisk,
     tooltip="v3: the source never states a padding distance — it says the stop goes 'just below that support' (11. 00:15). The pad is the word 'just'. In % mode it was 12.5 pts on NQ and 23.5 pts on YM, i.e. most of the whole max-stop budget. The tick is the one padding quantity module 4 actually defines, so ticks is the default. % mode is kept for reproducing v1/v2, not for use.")
padTicks  = input.float(1.0,  "Padding, ticks beyond the level", group=gRisk, minval=0,
     tooltip="1 tick = 0.25 pts on NQ, 1 pt on YM (4. 02:06 / 07:07). This is an INTERPRETATION of 'just below', not a stated rule — vary it in a forward test.")
stopPad   = input.float(0.05, "Padding, % of price (v1/v2 behaviour only)", group=gRisk)
useMaxStop= input.bool(true,  "Reject setup if stop too wide (11. 01:26)", group=gRisk)
instMode  = input.string("Auto from ticker", "Instrument", options=["Auto from ticker","NQ / NAS100","YM / US30"], group=gRisk,
     tooltip="Auto matches the ticker string. Per SYSTEM.md FINDING 15 a ticker string is never trusted silently, so the resolved instrument is printed on the dashboard — check it by eye.")
maxStopNQ = input.float(25.0, "Max stop, NQ / NAS100 (index points)", group=gRisk, tooltip="v3: 20 -> 25. 20 is Luca DESCRIBING Mamba (4. 04:09); module 11 is Mamba DEMONSTRATING — 'a 25 point stop loss, which is solid... actually a really good number' (00:20-00:23), 'we have a 25 point stop' (01:43), and 20 pts at (06:50). A 20-pt cap rejects his own worked example. Same demonstrated-over-described precedent as v2's trail-mode fix. The source brackets 20-25 and refuses 64 (01:26); it does not pin it.")
maxStopYM = input.float(30.0, "Max stop, YM / US30 (index points)", group=gRisk, tooltip="'let's say 30 points on YM' (4. 04:09). SINGLE-SOURCED and Luca-relayed — module 11 has no YM worked example, so unlike NQ there is no demonstrated number to prefer. Unchanged in v3.")
beAtR     = input.float(1.0,  "Move to break-even at R",               group=gRisk, tooltip="'at worst, at the least, please put your stops to break even at a one to one point five at the worst' (11. 08:02). 1.0 is the stated trigger; 1.5 is the stated latest.")
lockAtR   = input.float(2.0,  "Start locking profit at R",             group=gRisk)
trailMode = input.string("Freeze at +1R (what he demonstrates)", "Trail rule above break-even", options=["Break-even only (11. 09:01)","Freeze at +1R (what he demonstrates)","Step every +1R (11. 02:13)"], group=gRisk,
     tooltip="The source supports all three; only the break-even floor is non-negotiable. See the TRAIL note in this file's header.")
maxTrades = input.int(2,      "Max trades per day (counts across NQ+YM)", group=gRisk)

gTgt = "Target — 10._USING_DATA"
useAdaptR = input.bool(true, "Target = rolling mean achieved R (his actual rule)", group=gTgt)
rrWindow  = input.int(6, "Trades in the window", group=gTgt, minval=1, maxval=8, tooltip="'the last two weeks of trades, which is typically anywhere from six to eight trades' (10. 00:38). His worked example uses 6.")
rEstimator= input.string("Rolling last N trades (described, 10. 00:40)", "Achieved-R estimator", options=["Rolling last N trades (described, 10. 00:40)","Expanding mean from the first trade (demonstrated, 10. 05:11)"], group=gTgt,
     tooltip="v9, FINDING 27. The module gives TWO estimators four minutes apart and this file has only ever implemented one. DESCRIBED: 'I like to go over the last two weeks of trades' (00:40) — a rolling window. DEMONSTRATED: having computed 18.5/6 = 3.08, the next day's trade is folded in as 18.5 + 2 = 20.5 'divided by now SEVEN trades' (05:11) — the oldest trade is NOT dropped. He never states a drop rule at all, so v1-v8's drop-oldest-at-N is an interpretation, exactly as the trail mode and the stop pad were. Default is unchanged so the trade record still matches v8's. NOTE: this does NOT escape the ratchet in the header — every recorded R is capped at the target in force under both estimators, so the target is non-increasing either way. Switching it changes how FAST the target falls, not whether.")
rTarget   = input.float(3.0, "Fallback / fixed target, R", group=gTgt, tooltip="Used until the window has filled, or if the adaptive rule is switched off.")

gV = "Display"
showDash = input.bool(true, "Dashboard",        group=gV)
showLvls = input.bool(true, "S/R levels",       group=gV)
showStruct=input.bool(true, "Structure labels", group=gV)
showTrade= input.bool(true, "Trade levels",     group=gV)
showLadder=input.bool(true, "Target ladder 1R-5R (his 'target one..five')", group=gV)
showBrkt = input.bool(true, "Counter-structure bracket (what the streams show)", group=gV)

// ── 0. INSTRUMENT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// v4 moved this block up from the risk section: the touch tolerance now resolves against the live
// max-stop cap, so the instrument must be known before the level block runs. Reads inputs and
// `syminfo` only — no behaviour change from v3.
// v2: resolved per instrument. 30 pts is ~0.14% of NQ and ~0.07% of YM — one number cannot serve
// both, and v1 applied 30 to whatever chart it was on.
tkr   = str.upper(syminfo.ticker)
autoYM= str.contains(tkr, "YM") or str.contains(tkr, "US30") or str.contains(tkr, "DJI") or str.contains(tkr, "DOW")
isYM  = instMode == "YM / US30" or (instMode == "Auto from ticker" and autoYM)
instNm= isYM ? "YM / US30" : "NQ / NAS100"
maxStopPts = isYM ? maxStopYM : maxStopNQ

// ── 1. SESSION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// v5: the raw session test is kept separate from the gate. The volume-baseline instrumentation
// has to know what is really outside the New York session even when the session GATE is switched
// off, or turning `useSession` off would silently report a clean baseline.
inSessRaw = not na(time(timeframe.period, sessTime, sessTZ))
inSess  = not useSession or inSessRaw
newDay  = ta.change(time("1D")) != 0
// v8 CHECK, RECORDED BECAUSE A CLEAN RESULT IS ALSO A RESULT (FINDING 25 item 23): `newDay` was the
// other suspect in the session block. It is fine. A CME daily bar rolls at the Globex open
// (17:00/18:00 ET) and a cash-index daily bar at midnight; both are outside 09:30-16:00, so exactly
// one New York session falls inside each "day" on either instrument family, and neither the 2-trade
// cap nor the v5 session-to-date volume accumulator can be split across a session.

// ── 1b. THE TRADING WINDOW — v8, FINDING 25 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `sessTime`'s 1600 has never been sourced (module 8 states only a start), and the five NY streams
// are each over within ~20 minutes of the open. This measures where inside the session we are, so
// the entry window can be bounded by a switch and counted whether or not the switch is on.
var int sessOpenTime = na
if newDay
    sessOpenTime := na
if inSessRaw and na(sessOpenTime)
    sessOpenTime := time
minsSinceOpen = na(sessOpenTime) ? na : (time - sessOpenTime) / 60000.0
// OFF by default: with `entryCut` false this is always true and v8's signal set equals v7's.
inWindow = not entryCut or (not na(minsSinceOpen) and minsSinceOpen <= entryCutMins)

// ── 2. MARKET STRUCTURE — higher highs / higher lows vs lower lows / lower highs ──────────────
ph = ta.pivothigh(high, pivLen, pivLen)
pl = ta.pivotlow(low,   pivLen, pivLen)

var float lastPH = na, var float prevPH = na
var float lastPL = na, var float prevPL = na
var int   lastPHbar = na, var int lastPLbar = na       // v2: needed to exclude a level's own pivot window
var int   prevPHbar = na, var int prevPLbar = na       // v6: age instrumentation for FINDING 21

if not na(ph)
    prevPH := lastPH
    prevPHbar := lastPHbar
    lastPH := ph
    lastPHbar := bar_index - pivLen
if not na(pl)
    prevPL := lastPL
    prevPLbar := lastPLbar
    lastPL := pl
    lastPLbar := bar_index - pivLen

hh = not na(lastPH) and not na(prevPH) and lastPH > prevPH
lh = not na(lastPH) and not na(prevPH) and lastPH < prevPH
hl = not na(lastPL) and not na(prevPL) and lastPL > prevPL
ll = not na(lastPL) and not na(prevPL) and lastPL < prevPL

bullRaw = hh and hl             // "higher highs followed by higher lows"
bearRaw = ll and lh             // "lower low, lower high"

// ── 2b. STRUCTURE INVALIDATION — v6, FINDING 21 ───────────────────────────────────────────────
// v1-v5 had no invalidation rule of their own: a structure state simply persisted until the NEXT
// confirmed pivot changed one of the four comparisons. ta.pivotlow cannot confirm until pivLen
// bars after the swing low, so between the moment price broke the higher low and the moment the
// state changed there were AT LEAST pivLen bars — 25 min on 5m, 75 min on 15m — plus the whole
// length of the down leg, during which `bullStruct` was still true and the direction gate still
// permitted longs into a structure that had already broken.
//
// The source states the invalidation as a price event and never waits for a swing to complete:
//   "You see this level here. We did not break past the previous spot. So that previous higher
//    high and higher low we didn't break past that" (6. 03:30-03:35)
//   "now if price would have came down here and started to push down in this way BOOM that is now
//    a lower low" (6. 03:41)
//
// HARD LESSON 8 check, done before writing the latch: the arming event is a fresh confirmed pivot
// low, and ta.pivotlow guarantees the pivLen bars after that low all have HIGHER lows, so the
// confirming bar's close is necessarily above lastPL. The latch therefore cannot be killed by the
// same price action that arms it — the failure mode v16/v17 of 3M Elite died on.
var bool bullDead = false
var bool bearDead = false
if not na(pl)
    bullDead := false                       // a fresh higher/lower low re-arms the bull reading
if not na(ph)
    bearDead := false
if not na(lastPL) and close < lastPL
    bullDead := true                        // price broke the higher low the bull count sits on
if not na(lastPH) and close > lastPH
    bearDead := true                        // price broke the lower high the bear count sits on

useInval   = structInval == "Price breaks the defining swing (6. 03:35)"
bullStruct = bullRaw and not (useInval and bullDead)
bearStruct = bearRaw and not (useInval and bearDead)
structKilled = useInval and ((bullRaw and bullDead) or (bearRaw and bearDead))
consolidating = not bullStruct and not bearStruct   // "the gray zone... we don't trade it"

// v6 instrumentation — the two arithmetic claims of FINDING 21, measured on the chart instead of
// argued. (a) how old the four pivots that define the state actually are, and (b) how many of them
// formed before today's New York open. On 15m the state CANNOT be built from inside the session:
// the minimum is 2*pivLen + (pivLen+1) + 1 = 17 bars and a realistic four-swing sequence is ~29,
// against 390/15 = 26 session bars. This row says whether that is happening, it does not assume it.
var int sessStartBar = na
if inSessRaw and not nz(inSessRaw[1], false)
    sessStartBar := bar_index
f_preSess(b) => na(b) or na(sessStartBar) ? 0 : (b < sessStartBar ? 1 : 0)
structDefined = not na(lastPH) and not na(prevPH) and not na(lastPL) and not na(prevPL)
float ageOldest = structDefined ? math.max(math.max(bar_index - lastPHbar, bar_index - prevPHbar),
                                           math.max(bar_index - lastPLbar, bar_index - prevPLbar)) : na
ageOldMins= na(ageOldest) ? na : ageOldest * timeframe.in_seconds() / 60.0
nPreSess  = f_preSess(lastPHbar) + f_preSess(prevPHbar) + f_preSess(lastPLbar) + f_preSess(prevPLbar)
minStructBars = 2 * pivLen + (pivLen + 1) + 1
sessBarsTf    = math.round(390.0 / (timeframe.in_seconds() / 60.0))
invalPx   = bullRaw ? lastPL : bearRaw ? lastPH : na
invalDist = na(invalPx) ? na : math.abs(close - invalPx)

if showStruct and not na(ph)
    label.new(bar_index - pivLen, ph, hh ? "HH" : lh ? "LH" : "H", style=label.style_label_down,
         color=color.new(hh ? color.green : color.red, 40), textcolor=color.white, size=size.tiny)
if showStruct and not na(pl)
    label.new(bar_index - pivLen, pl, hl ? "HL" : ll ? "LL" : "L", style=label.style_label_up,
         color=color.new(hl ? color.green : color.red, 40), textcolor=color.white, size=size.tiny)

// ── 3. LEVELS — validated by DISTINCT touches, excluding the level's own pivot window ─────────
// v1 counted every BAR inside the tolerance band, over a window that included the pivot's own
// formation neighbourhood — so a level was "validated" the moment it formed, with no revisits.
// He counts visits: "one touch two touch three four five six seven touches with those wicks"
// (7. 00:47) and "one touch two touch three touch four touch almost another touch" (7. 01:04).
// A visit = an entry into the band from outside it. Both counts are returned so the correction's
// magnitude is visible rather than asserted.
// v4: the band is now in the cap's units. In % mode it was 24.95 pts on NQ and 46.94 on YM at his
// own screen prices — wider than the stop budget those same levels have to carry, so the counter
// and the stop-width gate were describing the same level with numbers 2-3× apart. See FINDING 19.
// % mode is left byte-for-byte as v1-v3, close-anchoring defect included, so the diff is clean.
tol = tolMode == "% of price (v1-v3 behaviour)" ? close * touchTol / 100 : tolFrac * maxStopPts

f_touches(lvl, lvlBar) =>
    int nRaw  = 0
    int nDist = 0
    bool inBand = false
    if not na(lvl)
        for i = 199 to 0
            bi = bar_index - i
            skip = bi < 0 or (not na(lvlBar) and math.abs(bi - lvlBar) <= pivLen)
            if not skip
                near = math.abs(high[i] - lvl) <= tol or math.abs(low[i] - lvl) <= tol
                if near
                    nRaw += 1
                    if not inBand
                        nDist += 1
                        inBand := true
                else
                    inBand := false
    [nRaw, nDist]

resLvl = lastPH
supLvl = lastPL
[resRaw, resDist] = f_touches(resLvl, lastPHbar)
[supRaw, supDist] = f_touches(supLvl, lastPLbar)

useDist  = touchMode == "Distinct visits (v2, his definition)"
resCount = useDist ? resDist : resRaw
supCount = useDist ? supDist : supRaw
resValid = not na(resLvl) and resCount >= minTouch
supValid = not na(supLvl) and supCount >= minTouch

plot(showLvls and resValid ? resLvl : na, "Resistance", color=color.new(color.red,  0), style=plot.style_stepline, linewidth=2)
plot(showLvls and supValid ? supLvl : na, "Support",    color=color.new(color.green,0), style=plot.style_stepline, linewidth=2)

// ── 3b. THE ROLE FLIP — v1 advertised it in the header and never implemented it ───────────────
// "But guess what support resistance does it turns into each other?" (7. 01:51)
// "So support broke and then became resistance... they do very often" (7. 02:16-02:19)
// "Resistance now support... a break and a retest of support into resistance" (7. 07:14)
// A flipped level stays live until price closes back through it (the reclaim invalidates it).
var float flipRes = na, var float flipSup = na
// v4: `breakClears` (OFF by default) requires the close to clear the tolerance band, not merely
// the pivot price. With it off — the default, unchanged from v1-v3 — a close one tick past the
// pivot is a break on a bar the counter simultaneously scores as a touch of that same level.
resBrkPx = na(resLvl) ? na : resLvl + (breakClears ? tol : 0.0)
supBrkPx = na(supLvl) ? na : supLvl - (breakClears ? tol : 0.0)
brokeResRaw = resValid and ta.crossover(close,  resBrkPx)
brokeSupRaw = supValid and ta.crossunder(close, supBrkPx)
if brokeSupRaw
    flipRes := supLvl        // a broken floor becomes a ceiling
if brokeResRaw
    flipSup := resLvl        // a broken ceiling becomes a floor
if not na(flipRes) and close > flipRes
    flipRes := na
if not na(flipSup) and close < flipSup
    flipSup := na

plot(showFlip and not na(flipRes) ? flipRes : na, "Flipped resistance (was support)", color=color.new(color.orange, 20), style=plot.style_stepline, linewidth=1)
plot(showFlip and not na(flipSup) ? flipSup : na, "Flipped support (was resistance)", color=color.new(color.aqua,   20), style=plot.style_stepline, linewidth=1)

// ── 4. BREAK ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Optional: a flipped level may also carry a break. OFF by default — see the input tooltip.
brokeFlipL = flipTrades and not na(flipSup) and ta.crossover(close,  flipSup + (breakClears ? tol : 0.0))
brokeFlipS = flipTrades and not na(flipRes) and ta.crossunder(close, flipRes - (breakClears ? tol : 0.0))
brokeRes = brokeResRaw or brokeFlipL
brokeSup = brokeSupRaw or brokeFlipS
// the level a long/short would actually be stopped behind
brkLvlLong  = brokeFlipL and not brokeResRaw ? flipSup : resLvl
brkLvlShort = brokeFlipS and not brokeSupRaw ? flipRes : supLvl

// ── 5. VOLUME ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// v5 (tick #11 — SYSTEM.md FINDING 20). Three things are wrong in this block and NOT ONE of them
// is the threshold: the baseline is a bar count that means two different durations on his two
// timeframes and is overnight for most of the 15m session; the quantity is a histogram test the
// source never uses; and the source's two actual volume BEHAVIOURS (an early exit and conviction
// sizing) were absent from the code entirely. See the v5 header note.
//
// THE DEFAULT BASELINE IS UNCHANGED. With default inputs this block returns exactly what v4's did,
// so v5's signal set and trade record are identical to v4's and the diff is auditable.
volAvg = ta.sma(volume, volLen)

// Session-to-date baseline (v5 option): the mean of the PRIOR in-session bars of the current day.
// Excluding the current bar is not a detail — including it makes the first session bar's ratio
// exactly 1.0, which would block the 09:30 entry the whole method is built around.
var float sessVolSum = 0.0
var int   sessVolCnt = 0
if newDay
    sessVolSum := 0.0
    sessVolCnt := 0
volAvgSessPrev = sessVolCnt > 0 ? sessVolSum / sessVolCnt : na
if inSessRaw
    sessVolSum += volume
    sessVolCnt += 1

useSessBase  = volBaseline == "Session-to-date average (v5 option)"
sessBaseLive = useSessBase and not na(volAvgSessPrev)   // falls back to the SMA on the day's first bar
volBase = sessBaseLive ? volAvgSessPrev : volAvg
volOK   = not useVol or (not na(volBase) and volBase > 0 and volume > volBase * volMult)
volRat  = (na(volBase) or volBase == 0) ? na : volume / volBase

// v5 instrumentation — how much of the trailing baseline is not the session at all. 20 bars is
// 100 min on 5m and 300 min on 15m, against a 390-min session, so this row reads very differently
// on his two timeframes and that difference is the finding.
volOutBars  = math.sum(inSessRaw ? 0.0 : 1.0, volLen)
volBaseMins = volLen * timeframe.in_seconds() / 60.0

// Conviction (9. 09:45-09:58) — a stated rule with no representation in v1-v4. DISPLAY ONLY: this
// file is an indicator, it takes no positions, `volGoodMult` is UNSOURCED, and this never feeds
// volOK or any signal. "Not a tighter stop loss — my contract size is going to get smaller."
convTxt = not volOK   ? "NO TRADE — no volume (9. 07:51)"
     : na(volRat)     ? "—"
     : volRat >= volGoodMult ? "FULL SIZE"
     : "REDUCED SIZE — smaller contracts, NOT a tighter stop (9. 09:55)"

// ── DAILY TRADE COUNTER — "up to two trades max per day" ─────────────────────────────────────
var int tradesToday = 0
if newDay
    tradesToday := 0
underCap = tradesToday < maxTrades

// ── ROLLING ACHIEVED-R WINDOW — 10._USING_DATA, the rule that actually sets the target ───────
// "I only calculated the second number. I don't care about the one." (10. 02:48)
// "We take the three, ZERO FOR A LOSS, two point five, five, five and a three." (10. 02:56)
// "That equals 18.5. We divide that by six... we are averaging a one, two, three." (10. 03:47)
// Eight shift registers rather than an array: arrays are on this project's forbidden list.
// NOTE: he says "median" (10. 00:52, 05:59) and computes the arithmetic MEAN every time. His own
// example cannot tell them apart ({0,2.5,3,3,5,5}: mean 3.083, median 3.0, both called "one to
// three"). Mechanising the arithmetic he performs, not the word he uses. See SYSTEM.md FINDING 7.
var float r1 = na, var float r2 = na, var float r3 = na, var float r4 = na
var float r5 = na, var float r6 = na, var float r7 = na, var float r8 = na

sumR = (rrWindow >= 1 and not na(r1) ? r1 : 0.0) + (rrWindow >= 2 and not na(r2) ? r2 : 0.0) +
       (rrWindow >= 3 and not na(r3) ? r3 : 0.0) + (rrWindow >= 4 and not na(r4) ? r4 : 0.0) +
       (rrWindow >= 5 and not na(r5) ? r5 : 0.0) + (rrWindow >= 6 and not na(r6) ? r6 : 0.0) +
       (rrWindow >= 7 and not na(r7) ? r7 : 0.0) + (rrWindow >= 8 and not na(r8) ? r8 : 0.0)
cntR = (rrWindow >= 1 and not na(r1) ? 1 : 0) + (rrWindow >= 2 and not na(r2) ? 1 : 0) +
       (rrWindow >= 3 and not na(r3) ? 1 : 0) + (rrWindow >= 4 and not na(r4) ? 1 : 0) +
       (rrWindow >= 5 and not na(r5) ? 1 : 0) + (rrWindow >= 6 and not na(r6) ? 1 : 0) +
       (rrWindow >= 7 and not na(r7) ? 1 : 0) + (rrWindow >= 8 and not na(r8) ? 1 : 0)

// ── v9: THE EXPANDING ESTIMATOR HE ACTUALLY DEMONSTRATES (FINDING 27) ─────────────────────────
// "if we had 18.5 originally... we just hit a two. So we add plus two... That equals 20.5. We would
// then divide that by now SEVEN trades and see we're down to 2.9." (10. 04:58-05:14)
// He adds the new trade and increments the denominator. Nothing is dropped, and no drop rule is
// stated anywhere in the module. v1-v8's drop-oldest-at-`rrWindow` is therefore an interpretation
// of the "last two weeks" phrasing, not a rule from the source. Both are now selectable.
var float cumSumR = 0.0
var int   cumCntR = 0
useExpand = rEstimator == "Expanding mean from the first trade (demonstrated, 10. 05:11)"

avgAchRoll = cntR > 0 ? sumR / cntR : na
avgAchExp  = cumCntR > 0 ? cumSumR / cumCntR : na
avgAchR    = useExpand ? avgAchExp : avgAchRoll
nInEst     = useExpand ? cumCntR : cntR
windowFull = useExpand ? cumCntR >= rrWindow : cntR >= rrWindow
// "we are averaging a one, two, three... we are going to go for one, two, threes" — rounded to a
// whole R, floored at 1 so a bad window cannot produce a sub-1R target he never trades.
rTargetUse = useAdaptR and windowFull and not na(avgAchR) ? math.max(1.0, math.round(avgAchR)) : rTarget

// ── v9 INSTRUMENTATION — THE RATCHET (FINDING 27). Drives nothing. ────────────────────────────
// The theorem in the header, made readable on one chart. `holdMean` is the mean the estimator must
// keep to hold the CURRENT target through the next recomputation (round(x) = T needs x >= T-0.5);
// `holdWR` is the same condition expressed as the win rate it implies if every win books the full
// target and every loss books 0 — which is exactly what this simulator produces. `beWR` is the win
// rate at which the same target merely breaks even in money. The gap between them is the finding.
var int   nWinT    = 0        // closed trades whose recorded R was > 0
var int   nLossT   = 0        // closed trades that scored 0 in the window (stop, break-even, cut)
var int   nRatchet = 0        // times the adaptive target has stepped DOWN on this chart
var float maxRtrade = na      // furthest R this trade has reached (capped at tgtR by the exit)
var float lastMaxR  = na      // and the same for the last CLOSED trade, for the ceiling row
var float capGap    = na      // (target it was opened against) − (furthest R it reached), last trade
holdMean = rTargetUse - 0.5
holdWR   = rTargetUse <= 0 ? na : 100 * (1 - 0.5 / rTargetUse)
beWR     = 100 / (1 + rTargetUse)
obsWR    = (nWinT + nLossT) > 0 ? 100.0 * nWinT / (nWinT + nLossT) : na
if useAdaptR and not na(rTargetUse[1]) and rTargetUse < rTargetUse[1]
    nRatchet := nRatchet + 1
absorbed = useAdaptR and windowFull and rTargetUse <= 1.0

// ── THE STOP-WIDTH GATE — a setup whose structure sits too far away is REJECTED, not re-stopped ─
// "That's a 25 point stop loss, which is solid." (11. 00:07)
// "64 points. No, my account's gone if I do that." (11. 01:26)
// The instrument and its cap are resolved in section 0 — v4 moved that block above the level
// block because the touch tolerance now depends on the cap.
//
// v3: ONE definition of the pad, in the cap's own units by default. v2 wrote this expression
// twice — here and inline in the trade-open block — so the two could silently diverge.
// In % mode the pad is 12.48 pts at NQ 24,954.50 and 23.47 pts at YM 46,942 (both read off his
// own screen, 4. 03:06 / 07:23), i.e. 62% and 78% of the entire max-stop budget before any
// structural distance is counted, rising with price until pad >= cap makes the gate impossible.
padNow      = padMode == "% of price (v1/v2 behaviour)" ? close * stopPad / 100 : padTicks * syminfo.mintick
padPct      = maxStopPts <= 0 ? na : 100 * padNow / maxStopPts   // share of the cap the pad eats
stopBudget  = maxStopPts - padNow                                // pts left for (entry - level)
projStopL   = na(brkLvlLong)  ? na : brkLvlLong  - padNow   // long stops below the broken level
projStopS   = na(brkLvlShort) ? na : brkLvlShort + padNow   // short stops above the broken level
projRlong   = na(projStopL) ? na : math.abs(close - projStopL)
projRshort  = na(projStopS) ? na : math.abs(close - projStopS)
stopOKlong  = not useMaxStop or (not na(projRlong)  and projRlong  <= maxStopPts)
stopOKshort = not useMaxStop or (not na(projRshort) and projRshort <= maxStopPts)

// v4 instrumentation — the commensurability test between the two gates. `tol` is the level's own
// positional uncertainty; `stopBudget` is the most a break may sit beyond that level and still be
// accepted. If tol > stopBudget then every tradeable break lies inside the band the counter still
// calls a touch, which is what v1-v3 shipped on both instruments (−0.20 pts NQ, −17.94 pts YM).
tolPct    = maxStopPts <= 0 ? na : 100 * tol / maxStopPts     // half-band as a share of the cap
bandPts   = 2 * tol                                           // full band width
tolFitsBudget = tol <= stopBudget

// ── THE SIGNAL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// v8: `inWindow` is identically true unless `entryCut` is switched on, so the default signal set is
// unchanged. It sits next to `inSess` because it is the same gate at a finer resolution — the
// session module states where the day STARTS and never where it ends (FINDING 25).
goLong  = inSess and inWindow and bullStruct and brokeRes and volOK and underCap and stopOKlong
goShort = inSess and inWindow and bearStruct and brokeSup and volOK and underCap and stopOKshort

// The break he would have marked on the OTHER side, which the course's structure gate forbids.
// Drawn, never traded — see the header note on the stated-vs-observed contradiction.
brktLvl = bullStruct ? supLvl : bearStruct ? resLvl : na
plot(showBrkt and not na(brktLvl) ? brktLvl : na, "Counter-structure bracket",
     color=color.new(color.gray, 35), style=plot.style_circles, linewidth=1)

// ── SIMULATED TRADE — so stop, target and the trail rule can be drawn ────────────────────────
var int   dir     = 0
var float entryPx = na, var float stopPx = na, var float tgtPx = na, var float initR = na
var float tgtR    = na                       // the target THIS trade was opened against
var line  lnE = na, var line  lnS = na, var line  lnT = na
var line  lad1 = na, var line lad2 = na, var line lad3 = na, var line lad4 = na, var line lad5 = na

curR = dir == 0 or na(initR) or initR == 0 ? na : (dir == 1 ? (close - entryPx) : (entryPx - close)) / initR
// v2 fix: manage on the PREVIOUS bar's R. v1 moved the stop using this bar's close and then
// tested this bar's low against it, booking exits on lows that preceded the move that caused them.
curRprev = dir == 0 or na(initR) or initR == 0 ? na : (dir == 1 ? (close[1] - entryPx) : (entryPx - close[1])) / initR

// v9 instrumentation (FINDING 27). The furthest R this trade has reached, using the bar EXTREME
// rather than the close, so it is the most generous reading of "the risk to reward I was able to
// capture" (10. 00:58) this file can produce. It is recorded to demonstrate the ceiling, not to
// escape it: the trade closes when the target is touched, so this quantity is itself capped at
// `tgtR` (plus at most one bar's overshoot) and CANNOT lift the estimator above the target. That is
// the point — no choice of what to feed the window escapes a simulator that exits at the target.
if dir != 0 and not na(initR) and initR != 0
    float exc = (dir == 1 ? (high - entryPx) : (entryPx - low)) / initR
    maxRtrade := na(maxRtrade) ? exc : math.max(maxRtrade, exc)

// manage the stop — see the TRAIL note in the header; the mode is an input because the source
// genuinely supports three readings and only the break-even floor is stated as non-negotiable.
if dir != 0 and not na(curRprev)
    float wantR = na
    if curRprev >= beAtR
        wantR := 0.0                                              // break-even (11. 02:28 / 08:02)
    if trailMode != "Break-even only (11. 09:01)" and curRprev >= lockAtR
        wantR := 1.0                                              // lock +1R (11. 03:21 / 06:38)
    if trailMode == "Step every +1R (11. 02:13)" and curRprev >= lockAtR
        wantR := math.max(1.0, math.floor(curRprev) - 1.0)         // step with it (11. 02:13 / 03:59)
    if not na(wantR)
        newStop = dir == 1 ? entryPx + wantR * initR : entryPx - wantR * initR
        stopPx := dir == 1 ? math.max(stopPx, newStop) : math.min(stopPx, newStop)

// v5: the run of consecutive bars inside the trade that fail the volume test. Reset while flat so
// a stale run cannot carry across into the next trade.
var int deadRun = 0
deadRun := dir == 0 ? 0 : (volOK ? 0 : deadRun + 1)

// ── v7: TRADE RESOLUTION vs BAR RESOLUTION (tick #14 — SYSTEM.md FINDING 23) ───────────────────
// PURE INSTRUMENTATION. Nothing below feeds a signal, an exit or the rolling-R window.
// The corpus records his ladder being traversed in seconds-to-minutes on every one of the five NY
// streams. This counts what the simulator actually experiences, in the same units, so the mismatch
// is a number on the chart rather than an argument in a markdown file.
var int   barsInTrade   = 0
var int   lastTradeBars = na
var float sumTradeBars  = 0.0
var int   nClosedT      = 0
var int   nEntriesT     = 0
var int   nTgtInBarT    = 0
var int   nBothInBarT   = 0
barsInTrade := dir == 0 ? 0 : barsInTrade + 1
tfMins      = timeframe.in_seconds() / 60.0

// ── v8: THE TRADING WINDOW (tick #15 — SYSTEM.md FINDING 25) ──────────────────────────────────
// Counters first, so they are live for the exit blocks below. All three are pure instrumentation
// and are counted whether or not `entryCut`/`flatEOD` are switched on — that is the point of them.
var int barsOOS    = 0     // bars of the LIVE trade that sat outside the New York session
var int nHeldOOS   = 0     // closed trades that were held across at least one out-of-session bar
var int nEntryLate = 0     // entries taken more than `entryCutMins` after the open
var int nEODflat   = 0     // trades closed by the session-close flatten (only when flatEOD is on)
var float lastEntryMins = na
barsOOS := dir == 0 ? 0 : (inSessRaw ? barsOOS : barsOOS + 1)

// THE SESSION-CLOSE FLATTEN. Evaluated BEFORE the stop/target block on purpose: it books at the
// last in-session close, which chronologically PRECEDES this bar's range, so testing this bar's
// high/low first would order the two exits backwards. `newDay` covers a cash-index feed that has
// no out-of-session bars at all (every bar is RTH, so the session never "just ended" — the next
// bar is simply the next day's open); the second clause covers a futures feed that does.
// OFF by default. With `flatEOD` false nothing here fires and the trade record equals v7's.
sessJustEnded = (not inSessRaw and inSessRaw[1]) or newDay
if flatEOD and dir != 0 and sessJustEnded and not na(close[1]) and not na(initR) and initR != 0
    outR = (dir == 1 ? (close[1] - entryPx) : (entryPx - close[1])) / initR
    label.new(bar_index, dir == 1 ? low : high,
         "EOD FLAT " + str.tostring(outR, "#.##") + "R  (5. 00:27)",
         style = dir == 1 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down,
         color = color.new(color.orange, 20), textcolor=color.white, size=size.small)
    if not na(lnE)
        line.set_x2(lnE, bar_index), line.set_x2(lnS, bar_index), line.set_x2(lnT, bar_index)
    if not na(lad1)
        line.set_x2(lad1, bar_index), line.set_x2(lad2, bar_index), line.set_x2(lad3, bar_index)
        line.set_x2(lad4, bar_index), line.set_x2(lad5, bar_index)
    r8 := r7
    r7 := r6
    r6 := r5
    r5 := r4
    r4 := r3
    r3 := r2
    r2 := r1
    r1 := math.max(outR, 0)          // a loss scores 0, exactly as a full stop does (10. 02:56)
    cumSumR   := cumSumR + math.max(outR, 0)     // v9: the expanding estimator (10. 05:11)
    cumCntR   := cumCntR + 1
    nWinT     := nWinT  + (outR > 0.001 ? 1 : 0) // v9 instrumentation only
    nLossT    := nLossT + (outR > 0.001 ? 0 : 1)
    capGap    := (na(maxRtrade) or na(tgtR)) ? na : tgtR - maxRtrade
    lastMaxR  := maxRtrade
    maxRtrade := na
    lastTradeBars := barsInTrade
    sumTradeBars  := sumTradeBars + barsInTrade
    nClosedT      := nClosedT + 1
    nEODflat      := nEODflat + 1
    if barsOOS > 0
        nHeldOOS := nHeldOOS + 1
    dir := 0

hitT = dir ==  1 and high >= tgtPx  or dir == -1 and low  <= tgtPx
hitS = dir ==  1 and low  <= stopPx or dir == -1 and high >= stopPx

if dir != 0 and (hitT or hitS)
    // v2 fix: an ambiguous bar (both touched) books the STOP, not the target. Achieved R feeds the
    // rolling-mean target, so an optimistic tie-break biases the target upward on every such bar.
    outR = hitS ? (dir == 1 ? (stopPx - entryPx) : (entryPx - stopPx)) / initR : tgtR
    txt  = not hitS ? "TARGET +" + str.tostring(tgtR, "#.#") + "R"
             : outR > 0.001  ? "TRAIL +" + str.tostring(outR, "#.#") + "R"
             : outR > -0.001 ? "BREAK-EVEN" : "STOP −1R"
    label.new(bar_index, dir == 1 ? high : low, txt, style = dir == 1 ? label.style_label_down : label.style_label_up,
         color = outR > 0 ? color.new(color.teal, 20) : color.new(color.maroon, 20), textcolor=color.white, size=size.small)
    if not na(lnE)
        line.set_x2(lnE, bar_index), line.set_x2(lnS, bar_index), line.set_x2(lnT, bar_index)
    if not na(lad1)
        line.set_x2(lad1, bar_index), line.set_x2(lad2, bar_index), line.set_x2(lad3, bar_index)
        line.set_x2(lad4, bar_index), line.set_x2(lad5, bar_index)
    // push the achieved R into his rolling window — a loss scores 0, per 10. 02:56
    r8 := r7
    r7 := r6
    r6 := r5
    r5 := r4
    r4 := r3
    r3 := r2
    r2 := r1
    r1 := math.max(outR, 0)
    cumSumR   := cumSumR + math.max(outR, 0)     // v9: the expanding estimator (10. 05:11)
    cumCntR   := cumCntR + 1
    nWinT     := nWinT  + (outR > 0.001 ? 1 : 0) // v9 instrumentation only
    nLossT    := nLossT + (outR > 0.001 ? 0 : 1)
    capGap    := (na(maxRtrade) or na(tgtR)) ? na : tgtR - maxRtrade
    lastMaxR  := maxRtrade
    maxRtrade := na
    lastTradeBars := barsInTrade                 // v7 instrumentation only
    sumTradeBars  := sumTradeBars + barsInTrade
    nClosedT      := nClosedT + 1
    if barsOOS > 0                               // v8 instrumentation only
        nHeldOOS := nHeldOOS + 1
    dir := 0

// ── v5: THE VOLUME-DEATH EARLY EXIT (9. 03:22-03:38) ──────────────────────────────────────────
// "No volume doesn't look good. We're out of this trade. Yes, we're negative right now, but we
// would rather lose this small amount right here, which would be about seven points, then let it
// go all the way up and take us out for 15 points, more than double that. Just take your loss,
// cut it off early."  SYSTEM.md has carried this in the mechanical spec since tick #1 and v1-v4
// never implemented it — the same defect class as v2's role flip.
//
// Evaluated AFTER the stop/target block on purpose: an intrabar stop or target touch precedes the
// close this exit is taken at, and booking the stop on an ambiguous bar is the convention v2 fix
// #4 already set for this file.
//
// ⚠ THE INTERACTION THIS CREATES WITH HIS OWN TARGET RULE, derived in FINDING 20 and NOT measured:
// a loss scores ZERO in his rolling window regardless of its size (10. 02:56). So relative to
// letting the trade run, cutting early can only replace would-be WINNERS with zeros — the cut
// itself is invisible on the loss side of that computation. It saves real money and it can only
// push the rolling-mean target DOWN. Two of his own rules, pulling against each other.
volDead = dir != 0 and volDeadExit and deadRun >= volDeadBars and not na(curR) and curR < volDeadR
if volDead
    outR = curR
    label.new(bar_index, dir == 1 ? low : high,
         "VOL DEAD — cut " + str.tostring(outR, "#.##") + "R  (9. 03:22)",
         style = dir == 1 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down,
         color = color.new(color.purple, 20), textcolor=color.white, size=size.small)
    if not na(lnE)
        line.set_x2(lnE, bar_index), line.set_x2(lnS, bar_index), line.set_x2(lnT, bar_index)
    if not na(lad1)
        line.set_x2(lad1, bar_index), line.set_x2(lad2, bar_index), line.set_x2(lad3, bar_index)
        line.set_x2(lad4, bar_index), line.set_x2(lad5, bar_index)
    r8 := r7
    r7 := r6
    r6 := r5
    r5 := r4
    r4 := r3
    r3 := r2
    r2 := r1
    r1 := math.max(outR, 0)          // a loss scores 0, exactly as a full stop does (10. 02:56)
    cumSumR   := cumSumR + math.max(outR, 0)     // v9: the expanding estimator (10. 05:11)
    cumCntR   := cumCntR + 1
    nWinT     := nWinT  + (outR > 0.001 ? 1 : 0) // v9 instrumentation only
    nLossT    := nLossT + (outR > 0.001 ? 0 : 1)
    capGap    := (na(maxRtrade) or na(tgtR)) ? na : tgtR - maxRtrade
    lastMaxR  := maxRtrade
    maxRtrade := na
    lastTradeBars := barsInTrade                 // v7 instrumentation only
    sumTradeBars  := sumTradeBars + barsInTrade
    nClosedT      := nClosedT + 1
    if barsOOS > 0                               // v8 instrumentation only
        nHeldOOS := nHeldOOS + 1
    dir := 0

if dir == 0 and (goLong or goShort)
    dir     := goLong ? 1 : -1
    entryPx := close
    lvl      = goLong ? brkLvlLong : brkLvlShort
    pad      = padNow                             // v3: one definition, not a second copy of the formula
    stopPx  := goLong ? lvl - pad : lvl + pad     // "our stop loss is below where it would come
                                                  //  back to retest" (7. 05:43) — the broken level,
                                                  //  never the far one
    initR   := math.abs(entryPx - stopPx)
    tgtR    := rTargetUse
    tgtPx   := goLong ? entryPx + initR * tgtR : entryPx - initR * tgtR
    tradesToday += 1
    // v7 instrumentation (tick #14, FINDING 23) — DIAGNOSTIC ONLY, it drives nothing.
    // Does the ENTRY BAR's own range already span this trade's target, or its target AND its stop?
    // Entry is taken at that bar's close, so the part of the bar after entry is unknown and this is
    // NOT a claim the trade would have resolved there. It is a statement about the bar being coarse
    // relative to the trade's geometry — the exact condition FINDING 23 says the corpus implies is
    // normal rather than exceptional. Used for exits it would be lookahead; used as a counter it is
    // the cheapest available test of whether this timeframe can represent his trades at all.
    barTgt   = goLong ? high >= tgtPx  : low  <= tgtPx
    barStop  = goLong ? low  <= stopPx : high >= stopPx
    nEntriesT  := nEntriesT  + 1
    nTgtInBarT := nTgtInBarT + (barTgt ? 1 : 0)
    nBothInBarT:= nBothInBarT + (barTgt and barStop ? 1 : 0)
    // v8 instrumentation (tick #15, FINDING 25) — DIAGNOSTIC ONLY. Counted whether or not the
    // cutoff is switched on, so one chart says how many of this file's entries fall outside the
    // window the corpus actually records him trading in.
    lastEntryMins := minsSinceOpen
    if not na(minsSinceOpen) and minsSinceOpen > entryCutMins
        nEntryLate := nEntryLate + 1
    if showTrade
        lnE := line.new(bar_index, entryPx, bar_index+1, entryPx, color=color.blue,  width=2)
        lnS := line.new(bar_index, stopPx,  bar_index+1, stopPx,  color=color.red,   width=2, style=line.style_dashed)
        lnT := line.new(bar_index, tgtPx,   bar_index+1, tgtPx,   color=color.green, width=2, style=line.style_dashed)
    if showLadder
        // "target one... target two... target three... target four... target five" — all five NY
        // streams. He scales out along these rungs; 10._USING_DATA decides which one calls the day.
        // NOTE: this simulator exits in ONE piece at tgtR. The ladder is drawn, not traded — a
        // scale-out would make "achieved R" a blend, and nothing in the source says the weights.
        s = dir
        lad1 := line.new(bar_index, entryPx + s*initR*1, bar_index+1, entryPx + s*initR*1, color=color.new(color.olive,50), style=line.style_dotted)
        lad2 := line.new(bar_index, entryPx + s*initR*2, bar_index+1, entryPx + s*initR*2, color=color.new(color.olive,50), style=line.style_dotted)
        lad3 := line.new(bar_index, entryPx + s*initR*3, bar_index+1, entryPx + s*initR*3, color=color.new(color.olive,50), style=line.style_dotted)
        lad4 := line.new(bar_index, entryPx + s*initR*4, bar_index+1, entryPx + s*initR*4, color=color.new(color.olive,50), style=line.style_dotted)
        lad5 := line.new(bar_index, entryPx + s*initR*5, bar_index+1, entryPx + s*initR*5, color=color.new(color.olive,50), style=line.style_dotted)
    label.new(bar_index, goLong ? low : high,
         (goLong ? "▲ LONG break " : "▼ SHORT break ") + str.tostring(lvl, format.mintick) + "  #" + str.tostring(tradesToday),
         style = goLong ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down,
         color = goLong ? color.new(color.green,10) : color.new(color.red,10), textcolor=color.white)

if dir != 0 and not na(lnE)
    line.set_x2(lnE, bar_index), line.set_x2(lnS, bar_index), line.set_x2(lnT, bar_index)
    line.set_y1(lnS, stopPx), line.set_y2(lnS, stopPx)
if dir != 0 and not na(lad1)
    line.set_x2(lad1, bar_index), line.set_x2(lad2, bar_index), line.set_x2(lad3, bar_index)
    line.set_x2(lad4, bar_index), line.set_x2(lad5, bar_index)

// ── SHADING ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
bgcolor(useSession and not inSess ? color.new(color.gray, 90) : na,  title="Outside New York session")
bgcolor(inSess and consolidating  ? color.new(color.orange, 90) : na,title="Consolidation — no trade")
bgcolor(inSess and bullStruct     ? color.new(color.green, 94) : na, title="Bullish structure")
bgcolor(inSess and bearStruct     ? color.new(color.red, 94) : na,   title="Bearish structure")
barcolor(useVol and not volOK ? color.new(color.gray, 50) : na,      title="No volume")

// ── DASHBOARD ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
var table d = table.new(position.top_right, 3, 26, border_width=1, frame_width=1, frame_color=color.gray)
f_row(t, r, k, ok, v) =>
    table.cell(t, 0, r, k, text_color=color.white,  text_size=size.small, bgcolor=color.new(color.black,20), text_halign=text.align_left)
    table.cell(t, 1, r, ok ? "✔" : "✖", text_color=ok ? color.lime : color.red, text_size=size.small, bgcolor=color.new(color.black,20))
    table.cell(t, 2, r, v, text_color=color.silver, text_size=size.small, bgcolor=color.new(color.black,20), text_halign=text.align_left)

f_tch(lvl, dst, raw) =>
    na(lvl) ? "—" : str.tostring(lvl, format.mintick) + "   " + str.tostring(dst) + " visits / " + str.tostring(raw) + " bars-in-band  (need " + str.tostring(minTouch) + ")"

if showDash and barstate.islast
    st = bullStruct ? "BULLISH — buys only" : bearStruct ? "BEARISH — sells only" : "CONSOLIDATION"
    tfOK = timeframe.period == "5" or timeframe.period == "15"
    table.cell(d, 0, 0, "LEGACY FOREX", text_color=color.white, text_size=size.normal, bgcolor=color.new(color.blue,30), text_halign=text.align_left)
    table.cell(d, 1, 0, syminfo.ticker,  text_color=color.white, text_size=size.normal, bgcolor=color.new(color.blue,30))
    table.cell(d, 2, 0, dir==1?"IN LONG":dir==-1?"IN SHORT":"flat", text_color=color.white, text_size=size.normal, bgcolor=color.new(color.blue,30), text_halign=text.align_left)

    f_row(d, 1, "Session",    inSess,       inSess ? "New York — live" : "outside NY — no trades")
    f_row(d, 2, "Timeframe",  tfOK,         timeframe.period + "m" + (tfOK ? "" : "  ← he trades 5m/15m ONLY"))
    f_row(d, 3, "Instrument", true,         syminfo.ticker + "  → read as " + instNm + ", max stop " + str.tostring(maxStopPts, "#") + " pts")
    f_row(d, 4, "Structure",  not consolidating, st)
    // v6 instrumentation — FINDING 21's headline. v1-v5 could only kill a structure when the NEXT
    // pivot confirmed, i.e. never sooner than pivLen bars after the swing that broke it. This row
    // is the fifth zero-signal cause the dashboard can name, and it is the one most likely to be
    // the cause in chop, which is exactly why it is instrumented rather than assumed.
    f_row(d, 5, "Struct guard", not structKilled,
         not useInval ? "v1-v5 mode — no price invalidation (structure dies only on a new pivot)"
           : structKilled ? "INVALIDATED — price broke the " + (bullRaw ? "higher low" : "lower high")
                            + " at " + str.tostring(invalPx, format.mintick) + "; waits for a fresh pivot"
           : na(invalPx) ? "no structure to guard"
           : "alive — dies on a close " + (bullRaw ? "below " : "above ") + str.tostring(invalPx, format.mintick)
             + "  (" + str.tostring(invalDist, "#.#") + " pts away)")
    // v6 instrumentation — the arithmetic claim, measured. At pivLen 5 the code needs >= 17 bars
    // before any structure state can exist and ~29 for a realistic four-swing sequence, against 26
    // bars in a 15m New York session. If that is right, this row reads "4 of 4 formed pre-open".
    f_row(d, 6, "Struct age", structDefined and nPreSess == 0,
         not structDefined ? "not yet defined — needs 2 highs AND 2 lows"
           : "oldest defining pivot " + str.tostring(ageOldest, "#") + " bars / " + str.tostring(ageOldMins, "#")
             + " min ago  ·  " + str.tostring(nPreSess) + " of 4 formed BEFORE today's open"
             + "  ·  needs ≥" + str.tostring(minStructBars) + " bars, session is " + str.tostring(sessBarsTf, "#"))
    f_row(d, 7, "Resistance", resValid,     f_tch(resLvl, resDist, resRaw))
    f_row(d, 8, "Support",    supValid,     f_tch(supLvl, supDist, supRaw))
    f_row(d, 9, "Flipped",    not na(flipRes) or not na(flipSup),
         (na(flipRes) ? "" : "res " + str.tostring(flipRes, format.mintick) + "  ") +
         (na(flipSup) ? "" : "sup " + str.tostring(flipSup, format.mintick) + "  ") +
         (na(flipRes) and na(flipSup) ? "none live" : (flipTrades ? "(can fire)" : "(drawn only)")))
    f_row(d, 10, "Break",     brokeRes or brokeSup, brokeRes ? "broke RESISTANCE ✓" : brokeSup ? "broke SUPPORT ✓" : "waiting for a break")
    f_row(d, 11, "Volume",    volOK,        na(volRat) ? "—" : str.tostring(volRat, "#.##") + "× baseline  (need > " + str.tostring(volMult, "#.##") + ")")
    // v5 instrumentation — the baseline's own composition. 20 bars is 100 min on 5m and 300 on
    // 15m against a 390-min session, so at the open this row reads "20 of 20 outside the session"
    // on 15m. It is the fourth zero/near-inert-signal cause the dashboard can now name, and the
    // only one whose bias direction is signed: overnight volume is structurally lower, so a
    // contaminated baseline makes the gate LOOSER, not tighter.
    f_row(d, 12, "Vol baseline", sessBaseLive or volOutBars == 0,
         sessBaseLive ? "session-to-date, " + str.tostring(sessVolCnt) + " bars today  (v5 option)"
            : str.tostring(volLen) + " bars = " + str.tostring(volBaseMins, "#") + " min  →  "
              + str.tostring(volOutBars, "#") + " of them OUTSIDE the session"
              + (volOutBars > 0 ? "  ← partly overnight, gate is looser" : "  ← all in-session"))
    // v5: a stated three-state rule (9. 09:45-09:58) that v1-v4 rendered nowhere. Display only —
    // the threshold is printed because it is UNSOURCED and must read as a knob, not a finding.
    f_row(d, 13, "Conviction", volOK,
         convTxt + (na(volRat) ? "" : "   (full ≥ " + str.tostring(volGoodMult, "#.##") + "× — UNSOURCED, display only)"))
    // which side's stop width is the live question depends on which side structure permits
    projR    = bullStruct ? projRlong : bearStruct ? projRshort : na
    stopOKnow= bullStruct ? stopOKlong : bearStruct ? stopOKshort : true
    f_row(d, 14, "Stop width", stopOKnow, na(projR) ? "—" : str.tostring(projR, "#.#") + " pts  (max " + str.tostring(maxStopPts, "#") + " — he refused 64)")
    // v3 instrumentation: the pad and the cap were in different units, and the pad was eating most
    // of the budget. This row makes that visible so a zero-signal chart can be attributed to the
    // right gate instead of being confused with v2's touch-counting correction.
    f_row(d, 15, "Stop budget", stopBudget > 0,
         str.tostring(padNow, "#.##") + " pts pad (" + str.tostring(padPct, "#") + "% of cap)  →  "
         + (stopBudget > 0 ? str.tostring(stopBudget, "#.#") + " pts left for entry-to-level"
                           : "PAD ≥ CAP — no setup can ever pass"))
    // v4 instrumentation: the touch tolerance was a % of price while everything it has to be
    // commensurable with is in index points. This row is the third and last zero-signal cause on
    // the dashboard, so a blank chart can now be attributed rather than guessed at.
    f_row(d, 16, "Level width", tolFitsBudget,
         "±" + str.tostring(tol, "#.##") + " pts  (band " + str.tostring(bandPts, "#.#") + ", "
         + str.tostring(tolPct, "#") + "% of cap)  →  "
         + (tolFitsBudget ? "fits inside the " + str.tostring(stopBudget, "#.#") + "-pt stop budget"
                          : "TOL > BUDGET — every tradeable break is still inside the touch band"))
    // v9: `cntR` was hard-coded here; with the expanding estimator the count and the mean come from
    // a different accumulator, so both now read from `nInEst`/`avgAchR` and the estimator is named.
    f_row(d, 17, "Target",    true,      (useAdaptR ? (windowFull ? "1:" + str.tostring(rTargetUse, "#") + "  from mean " + str.tostring(avgAchR, "#.##") + "R of " + str.tostring(nInEst) + " trades  (" + (useExpand ? "EXPANDING, 10. 05:11" : "rolling, 10. 00:40") + ")"
                                                                  : "1:" + str.tostring(rTargetUse, "#") + "  FALLBACK — window " + str.tostring(nInEst) + "/" + str.tostring(rrWindow) + " trades")
                                                    : "1:" + str.tostring(rTargetUse, "#") + "  fixed (adaptive rule off)"))
    f_row(d, 18, "Trail rule", true,      trailMode + "   (source is ambiguous — see header)")
    f_row(d, 19, "Trades today", underCap, str.tostring(tradesToday) + " / " + str.tostring(maxTrades) + "  (cap counts across NQ+YM)")
    // v7 instrumentation (tick #14, FINDING 23). The corpus traverses 1R→4R in ~3 minutes on his
    // showcased days. This row says what that costs on THIS bar size. "✖" means at least one entry
    // bar already spanned both the target and the stop — a trade this timeframe cannot order, whose
    // outcome is then decided by v2 fix 4's tie-break convention rather than by price.
    f_row(d, 20, "Trade resolution", nBothInBarT == 0,
         (dir != 0 ? str.tostring(barsInTrade) + " bars held (" + str.tostring(barsInTrade * tfMins, "#") + " min)" : "flat")
         + (nClosedT > 0 ? "  ·  mean " + str.tostring(sumTradeBars / nClosedT, "#.#") + " bars ("
                           + str.tostring(sumTradeBars / nClosedT * tfMins, "#") + " min) over " + str.tostring(nClosedT) : "")
         + "  ·  entry bar already spans target " + str.tostring(nTgtInBarT) + "/" + str.tostring(nEntriesT)
         + ", target AND stop " + str.tostring(nBothInBarT))
    // v8 instrumentation (tick #15, FINDING 25). The session END has never been sourced — module 8
    // states only a start — and every day the corpus records is finished within ~20 minutes of it.
    // "✖" means at least one entry in this chart's history was taken beyond that window, i.e. the
    // file is signalling in a part of the day the source has no evidence he trades.
    f_row(d, 21, "Entry window", nEntryLate == 0,
         (na(minsSinceOpen) ? "pre-open" : str.tostring(minsSinceOpen, "#") + " min since the open")
         + "  ·  session is " + str.tostring(sessBarsTf, "#") + " bars / "
         + str.tostring(sessBarsTf * tfMins, "#") + " min"
         + "  ·  " + str.tostring(nEntryLate) + " of " + str.tostring(nEntriesT) + " entries beyond "
         + str.tostring(entryCutMins) + " min"
         + (entryCut ? "  (cutoff ON)" : "  (cutoff OFF — 1600 is UNSOURCED)"))
    // v8 instrumentation. v1-v7 had no time-based exit of any kind, so a trade could be carried
    // through the close and priced against overnight bars — and its R still fed the rolling-mean
    // target rule. "✖" means that has already happened on this chart.
    f_row(d, 22, "Hold window", nHeldOOS == 0,
         (dir != 0 and barsOOS > 0 ? "LIVE TRADE HELD " + str.tostring(barsOOS) + " bars out of session  ·  " : "")
         + str.tostring(nHeldOOS) + " of " + str.tostring(nClosedT) + " closed trades crossed the close"
         + (flatEOD ? "  ·  EOD flat ON, " + str.tostring(nEODflat) + " flattened"
                    : "  ·  EOD flat OFF — nothing closes the day (5. 00:27)"))
    // v9 instrumentation (tick #16, FINDING 27). THE RATCHET. "✖" means the adaptive target has
    // already stepped down on this chart, or has reached the absorbing 1R it can never leave.
    // `holdWR` is the win rate needed to HOLD the current target when every win books exactly the
    // target and every loss books 0 — which is what this simulator produces by construction; `beWR`
    // is the win rate at which that same target merely breaks even in money. The gap between the two
    // is the defect, and it widens as the target rises.
    f_row(d, 23, "Target ratchet", useAdaptR and nRatchet == 0 and not absorbed,
         not useAdaptR ? "adaptive rule OFF — fixed 1:" + str.tostring(rTarget, "#") + ", no feedback loop"
           : (absorbed ? "ABSORBED AT 1R — a 1R win books 1, so the mean can never reach the 1.5 needed to climb back"
                       : "holding 1:" + str.tostring(rTargetUse, "#") + " needs mean ≥ " + str.tostring(holdMean, "#.#")
                         + "R  (= " + str.tostring(holdWR, "#") + "% wins; break-even is " + str.tostring(beWR, "#") + "%)")
           + "  ·  observed " + (na(obsWR) ? "—" : str.tostring(obsWR, "#") + "% of " + str.tostring(nWinT + nLossT))
           + "  ·  stepped down " + str.tostring(nRatchet) + "×")
    // v9 instrumentation. WHY the ratchet is unescapable in this simulator: the trade closes AT the
    // target, so the furthest R it ever reaches is the target (plus at most one bar's overshoot).
    // His journal records what he CAPTURED (10. 00:58) and contains a 5 while he was going for a 3,
    // which is how his own rule climbs (10. 05:43). This row is that gap, measured. A `capGap` of
    // ~0 on every trade IS the ceiling — it is the expected reading, not a healthy one.
    f_row(d, 24, "Capture ceiling", false,
         "recorded R is capped at the target by construction — the ladder is DRAWN, not traded"
         + (na(lastMaxR) ? "" : "  ·  last closed trade reached " + str.tostring(lastMaxR, "#.##")
                                + "R, target minus that = " + str.tostring(capGap, "#.##") + "R"))

    // name the FIRST unmet condition, in his own order, so the dashboard says what is blocking
    blocker = not inSess        ? "NO TRADE — outside New York session"
      : not inWindow            ? "NO TRADE — past the " + str.tostring(entryCutMins) + "-minute entry window"
      : structKilled            ? "NO TRADE — structure INVALIDATED, price broke the swing it was built on (6. 03:35)"
      : consolidating           ? "NO TRADE — consolidation"
      : not (resValid or supValid) ? "WAIT — no level has " + str.tostring(minTouch) + " distinct touches yet"
      : not (brokeRes or brokeSup) ? "WAIT — no break of a validated level"
      : not volOK               ? "NO TRADE — no volume"
      : not stopOKnow           ? "NO TRADE — stop too wide (" + str.tostring(projR, "#.#") + " pts)"
      : not underCap            ? "NO TRADE — daily cap reached"
      : "WAIT"
    verdict = goLong ? "▲ LONG NOW" : goShort ? "▼ SHORT NOW"
      : dir != 0 ? ("HOLDING  " + (na(curR)?"":str.tostring(curR,"#.##") + "R  → 1:" + str.tostring(tgtR,"#"))
                    + (volDeadExit and not volOK ? "   [vol dead " + str.tostring(deadRun) + "/" + str.tostring(volDeadBars) + "]" : ""))
      : blocker
    vc = goLong ? color.green : goShort ? color.red : dir != 0 ? color.blue : color.new(color.gray,40)
    table.cell(d, 0, 25, "VERDICT", text_color=color.white, text_size=size.normal, bgcolor=vc, text_halign=text.align_left)
    table.cell(d, 1, 25, "", bgcolor=vc)
    table.cell(d, 2, 25, verdict, text_color=color.white, text_size=size.normal, bgcolor=vc, text_halign=text.align_left)

alertcondition(goLong,  "Legacy LONG",  "Legacy Forex: LONG — structure bullish, resistance broken, volume present")
alertcondition(goShort, "Legacy SHORT", "Legacy Forex: SHORT — structure bearish, support broken, volume present")

plot(goLong ? 1:0,  "LongSignal",  display=display.data_window)
plot(goShort ? 1:0, "ShortSignal", display=display.data_window)
plot(curR,          "CurrentR",    display=display.data_window)
plot(avgAchR,       "MeanAchievedR (10._USING_DATA)", display=display.data_window)
plot(rTargetUse,    "TargetR in use", display=display.data_window)
plot(bullStruct ? projRlong : bearStruct ? projRshort : na, "ProjectedStopPts", display=display.data_window)
// v2 instrumentation — the numbers a forward test needs to settle the touch-counting correction
plot(resDist,       "ResTouches distinct", display=display.data_window)
plot(resRaw,        "ResTouches bars-in-band (v1)", display=display.data_window)
plot(supDist,       "SupTouches distinct", display=display.data_window)
plot(supRaw,        "SupTouches bars-in-band (v1)", display=display.data_window)
// v3 instrumentation — separates the stop-width gate from the touch gate as a zero-signal cause
plot(padNow,        "Pad (pts)",              display=display.data_window)
plot(padPct,        "Pad as % of max stop",   display=display.data_window)
plot(stopBudget,    "Stop budget left (pts)", display=display.data_window)
// v4 instrumentation — the level's own width, in the same units as everything it must fit inside
plot(tol,           "Touch tolerance (pts, half-band)", display=display.data_window)
plot(bandPts,       "Touch band width (pts)",           display=display.data_window)
plot(tolPct,        "Tolerance as % of max stop",       display=display.data_window)
plot(tolFitsBudget ? 1 : 0, "TolFitsStopBudget",        display=display.data_window)
// v5 instrumentation — the volume gate. The first two settle FINDING 20's headline on one chart:
// how long the baseline window actually is in minutes, and how much of it is not the session.
plot(volBaseMins,   "Vol baseline span (minutes)",           display=display.data_window)
plot(volOutBars,    "Vol baseline bars outside session",     display=display.data_window)
plot(volRat,        "Volume vs baseline (×)",                display=display.data_window)
plot(volOK ? 1 : 0, "VolGatePass",                           display=display.data_window)
plot(sessBaseLive ? 1 : 0, "SessionBaselineLive",            display=display.data_window)
plot(deadRun,       "Consecutive no-volume bars in trade",   display=display.data_window)
// v6 instrumentation — FINDING 21. The first two settle the headline (how often the price-based
// invalidation fires, and how much of the time it is what is blocking a trade); the last three
// settle the arithmetic claim about pivLen against the session length, on the same chart.
plot(bullDead ? 1 : 0,     "BullStructInvalidated (v6)",          display=display.data_window)
plot(bearDead ? 1 : 0,     "BearStructInvalidated (v6)",          display=display.data_window)
plot(structKilled ? 1 : 0, "StructKilledThisBar (v6)",            display=display.data_window)
plot(invalDist,            "Points to structure invalidation",    display=display.data_window)
plot(ageOldest,            "Oldest defining pivot (bars ago)",    display=display.data_window)
plot(ageOldMins,           "Oldest defining pivot (minutes ago)", display=display.data_window)
plot(nPreSess,             "Defining pivots formed pre-open (0-4)", display=display.data_window)
plot(bullRaw ? 1 : 0,      "BullStruct before invalidation (v1-v5)", display=display.data_window)
plot(bearRaw ? 1 : 0,      "BearStruct before invalidation (v1-v5)", display=display.data_window)
// v7 instrumentation — FINDING 23. The corpus's holding period is minutes; this bar size is
// `tfMins` minutes. These six settle whether a 5m/15m simulator can represent his trade at all.
plot(barsInTrade,                  "Bars in trade",                        display=display.data_window)
plot(barsInTrade * tfMins,         "Minutes in trade",                     display=display.data_window)
plot(lastTradeBars,                "Bars in LAST closed trade",            display=display.data_window)
plot(nClosedT > 0 ? sumTradeBars / nClosedT : na, "Mean bars per closed trade", display=display.data_window)
plot(nTgtInBarT,                   "Entry bars already spanning target",   display=display.data_window)
plot(nBothInBarT,                  "Entry bars spanning target AND stop",  display=display.data_window)
// v8 instrumentation — FINDING 25. The first two say where inside the day this file is signalling;
// the last three say how much of its trade record lives outside the window the corpus supports.
plot(minsSinceOpen,                "Minutes since the New York open",      display=display.data_window)
plot(lastEntryMins,                "Minutes-after-open of the LAST entry", display=display.data_window)
plot(nEntryLate,                   "Entries beyond the cutoff",            display=display.data_window)
plot(barsOOS,                      "Live trade: bars held out of session", display=display.data_window)
plot(nHeldOOS,                     "Closed trades that crossed the close", display=display.data_window)
plot(nEODflat,                     "Trades flattened at the session close",display=display.data_window)
plot(inWindow ? 1 : 0,             "InEntryWindow",                        display=display.data_window)
// v9 instrumentation — FINDING 27. The first three settle the theorem on one chart: the target in
// use, the mean feeding it, and the mean it must keep to hold. The next three say how the two
// estimators differ on the same trade record. The last three are the capture ceiling — if
// `CapGapLastTrade` is ~0 on every closed trade, the recorded R is the target and the loop is shut.
plot(holdMean,                     "Mean needed to HOLD the target",       display=display.data_window)
plot(holdWR,                       "Win rate needed to hold target (%)",   display=display.data_window)
plot(beWR,                         "Break-even win rate at target (%)",    display=display.data_window)
plot(obsWR,                        "Observed win rate (%)",                display=display.data_window)
plot(nRatchet,                     "Times the target stepped DOWN",        display=display.data_window)
plot(avgAchRoll,                   "MeanAchievedR rolling (10. 00:40)",    display=display.data_window)
plot(avgAchExp,                    "MeanAchievedR expanding (10. 05:11)",  display=display.data_window)
plot(cumCntR,                      "Closed trades in the expanding mean",  display=display.data_window)
plot(maxRtrade,                    "Live trade: furthest R reached",       display=display.data_window)
plot(lastMaxR,                     "Last closed trade: furthest R reached",display=display.data_window)
plot(capGap,                       "CapGapLastTrade (target − furthest R)",display=display.data_window)
